window.ATLAS_CORE_LESSONS={"economics_fiscal_policy":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Fiscal Policy: how taxes and government spending move the economy","objective":"Explain how changes in government spending and taxes affect aggregate demand, output, employment, inflation, and the budget balance.","key_terms":[{"term":"Fiscal policy","definition":"Government decisions about spending, taxes, and transfers."},{"term":"Expansionary","definition":"Raises demand, usually through more spending, lower taxes, or larger transfers."},{"term":"Contractionary","definition":"Reduces demand, usually through less spending or higher taxes."},{"term":"Automatic stabilizer","definition":"A tax or transfer that changes automatically as the economy changes, such as unemployment benefits."}],"explanation":"Fiscal policy is one of the main ways a government can influence total spending in an economy. The basic mechanism is straightforward: households, firms, and governments all buy goods and services. If government purchases more goods and services, total demand rises directly. If taxes are cut or transfers rise, households usually have more disposable income, so some of that extra income is spent and demand rises indirectly.\n\nIn a recession, when factories, workers, and equipment are underused, stronger demand can raise production and employment because firms can respond by producing more. Near full capacity, however, the same increase in demand is more likely to push prices upward because the economy has less unused capacity. That is why the effect of fiscal policy depends on timing and economic conditions.\n\nFiscal policy also affects the government budget. Expansionary policy often increases the deficit in the short run; contractionary policy often reduces it. A deficit is not automatically good or bad. The important questions are what the spending or tax change does, when it occurs, how it is financed, and what alternatives were available.","worked_example":{"setup":"Suppose an economy is in a recession and businesses are selling less than they can produce.","steps":["The government funds $40 billion of infrastructure projects.","Construction firms hire workers and buy materials, so the first-round increase in demand is about $40 billion.","Workers and suppliers receiving that income spend part of it, creating additional rounds of demand.","If the total multiplier were 1.5, the eventual increase in aggregate demand would be roughly $60 billion, not because money appeared from nowhere, but because one person’s spending becomes another person’s income."],"result":"Output and employment may rise if the economy has spare capacity. The exact effect depends on how quickly the money is spent, the multiplier, interest rates, imports, and supply constraints."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Fiscal policy connects directly to inflation and unemployment through aggregate demand.","explanation":"When demand is weak, expansionary fiscal policy can raise production and hiring. When demand is already pressing against the economy’s productive capacity, additional demand can create more inflation pressure than extra output.","difference":"Inflation and unemployment can also move because of supply shocks, technology, labor-force changes, monetary policy, and many other forces. Fiscal policy is one influence, not a master control.","why_useful":"This connection lets you reason from a policy change to likely economic consequences instead of memorizing “spending up = good” or “deficit = bad.”"},"common_mistakes":["Treating every budget deficit as expansionary. A deficit can change because the economy weakened even without a new policy.","Assuming a dollar of government spending always creates exactly one dollar of GDP. Multiplier effects vary.","Ignoring capacity. The same demand boost can raise output in a recession but mostly raise prices near full capacity."],"takeaway":"Fiscal policy changes total demand through government spending, taxes, and transfers; its effects on jobs and prices depend heavily on the economy’s unused capacity and timing.","challenge":"A country has high unemployment, weak private investment, and low inflation. Explain one expansionary fiscal action, the first two steps in its causal chain, and one risk or limitation.","quiz":{"prompt":"The economy is in a deep recession with low inflation. Which action is most clearly expansionary fiscal policy?","options":["Increase government infrastructure spending","Raise the central bank policy rate","Require banks to hold more reserves","Reduce the money supply"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Infrastructure spending is a government budget decision that directly raises aggregate demand. The other choices are monetary or banking-policy actions.","feedback":["Correct. Government purchases directly enter aggregate demand.","A policy rate is monetary policy, not fiscal policy.","Reserve requirements are banking/monetary policy tools.","The money supply is controlled through monetary institutions, not the government budget."]}},"economics_inflation_and_unemployment":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Inflation & Unemployment: two signals of how the economy is using its capacity","objective":"Distinguish inflation from unemployment and explain why demand shocks and supply shocks can move them in different directions.","key_terms":[{"term":"Inflation","definition":"A sustained rise in the general price level, not simply one product becoming expensive."},{"term":"Unemployment rate","definition":"The share of the labor force without a job who are actively seeking work."},{"term":"Demand shock","definition":"A change in total desired spending."},{"term":"Supply shock","definition":"A change in the economy’s ability or cost to produce."}],"explanation":"Inflation and unemployment measure different parts of the economy. Inflation asks how quickly the overall price level is rising. Unemployment asks how many people in the labor force want work but do not have it.\n\nWhen total demand weakens, firms often sell less, cut production, and hire fewer workers. Unemployment can rise while inflation pressure falls. When demand strengthens in an economy with spare capacity, firms can increase production and hiring; unemployment may fall. If demand keeps rising after the economy is close to capacity, firms may have difficulty expanding output, so prices rise faster.\n\nSupply shocks behave differently. If energy, food, shipping, or other important inputs suddenly become more expensive or scarce, firms can face higher costs and lower production at the same time. That can produce the unpleasant combination of higher inflation and higher unemployment.","worked_example":{"setup":"Oil prices surge because a major supply source is disrupted.","steps":["Transportation and production costs rise for many businesses.","Some firms raise prices to protect margins, pushing inflation upward.","Other firms cut output because production is less profitable or demand falls after prices rise.","Reduced output can mean fewer hours, slower hiring, or layoffs."],"result":"Inflation and unemployment can rise together, showing why a simple permanent tradeoff between them does not exist."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Both variables help reveal whether changes are coming mostly from demand, supply, or a mix of both.","explanation":"Fiscal and monetary policy mainly influence demand, while technology, energy costs, labor supply, and productivity strongly affect supply.","difference":"A low unemployment rate does not itself prove inflation must rise, and high inflation does not identify its cause.","why_useful":"Reading both measures together prevents one-number explanations of the economy."},"common_mistakes":["Calling any price increase inflation. Inflation refers to a broad price level.","Counting everyone without a job as unemployed. People outside the labor force are classified separately.","Assuming inflation and unemployment must always move in opposite directions."],"takeaway":"Demand weakness often raises unemployment and reduces inflation pressure; supply disruptions can raise inflation while also weakening employment.","challenge":"Describe what you would expect to happen to inflation and unemployment after a large negative supply shock, and explain the mechanism.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which situation can plausibly produce both higher inflation and higher unemployment?","options":["A major negative energy supply shock","A broad productivity boom","A large increase in available workers with unchanged demand","A sharp fall in all production costs"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A negative supply shock raises costs and reduces output capacity, which can raise prices and weaken employment.","feedback":["Correct. This is the classic supply-shock mechanism.","A productivity boom tends to improve supply capacity.","More available labor does not by itself create the same cost-and-output shock.","Lower production costs generally reduce inflation pressure."]}},"economics_supply_and_demand":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Supply & Demand: why market prices move","objective":"Use supply and demand to explain how changes in buyers, sellers, costs, and preferences affect equilibrium price and quantity.","key_terms":[{"term":"Demand","definition":"How much buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices."},{"term":"Supply","definition":"How much sellers are willing and able to offer at different prices."},{"term":"Equilibrium","definition":"The price and quantity where desired purchases equal desired sales."}],"explanation":"Demand slopes downward in the basic model because, all else equal, buyers usually purchase less as price rises. Supply slopes upward because higher prices make additional production worthwhile for more sellers. The market price tends to move toward the point where the amount buyers want equals the amount sellers want to provide.\n\nA movement along a curve is caused by the good’s own price. A shift of the whole demand curve comes from something else, such as income, tastes, the number of buyers, or the price of substitutes. A shift of the supply curve can come from input costs, technology, taxes, regulations, weather, or the number of sellers.","worked_example":{"setup":"A freeze destroys part of the orange crop while consumer preferences are unchanged.","steps":["At every possible price, growers can now supply fewer oranges.","The supply curve shifts left.","At the old price there is a shortage because buyers want more oranges than sellers can provide.","Competition among buyers pushes the price upward until quantity demanded falls enough to meet the smaller quantity supplied."],"result":"Price rises and equilibrium quantity falls."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Supply and demand are the foundation for later topics such as elasticity, taxes, price controls, labor markets, and international trade.","explanation":"Those topics ask what happens when the curves move, how strongly buyers and sellers react, or how policy changes incentives.","difference":"The simple model assumes many real-world details are held constant. Market power, information problems, externalities, and institutions can change outcomes.","why_useful":"It gives you a causal starting point instead of treating price changes as arbitrary."},"common_mistakes":["Saying “demand increased” when only quantity demanded changed because price fell.","Forgetting to state which curve shifted and why."],"takeaway":"Price and quantity outcomes depend on where supply and demand meet; outside forces shift the curves, while the good’s own price moves you along a curve.","challenge":"A new manufacturing technology cuts the cost of producing batteries. Predict the direction of the supply shift, price change, and quantity change.","quiz":{"prompt":"If demand rises while supply is unchanged, what normally happens to equilibrium price and quantity?","options":["Both rise","Price falls and quantity rises","Price rises and quantity falls","Both fall"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A rightward demand shift creates excess demand at the old price, pushing both equilibrium price and quantity upward.","feedback":["Correct.","Quantity can rise, but price normally rises rather than falls.","A demand increase does not normally reduce equilibrium quantity.","Both falling would be more consistent with a demand decrease."]}},"economics_scarcity_and_opportunity_cost":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Scarcity & Opportunity Cost: the logic behind every economic choice","objective":"Explain why scarcity forces tradeoffs and calculate opportunity cost from the best forgone alternative.","key_terms":[{"term":"Scarcity","definition":"Resources are limited relative to the wants they could satisfy."},{"term":"Opportunity cost","definition":"The value of the best alternative you give up when you choose."}],"explanation":"Scarcity does not mean something is rare in an absolute sense. It means time, money, labor, land, materials, and attention are limited, so using a resource one way prevents using that same resource another way. Every choice therefore has an opportunity cost.\n\nOpportunity cost is not the sum of every option you reject. It is the value of the single best alternative you would otherwise have chosen. This idea applies to households, firms, and governments.","worked_example":{"setup":"You have two free hours. Your best options are working overtime for $60, studying for an exam, or watching a movie.","steps":["Suppose you choose to study.","If your next-best alternative was earning $60 overtime, that $60 is part of the opportunity cost of studying.","The movie is not added unless it was actually the best alternative you would have chosen instead."],"result":"The opportunity cost is the value of the best forgone option, not every forgone option."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Scarcity creates the tradeoffs that supply, demand, budgeting, investment, and policy analysis all try to manage.","explanation":"Whenever Atlas asks “what did this decision cost?” the economic answer includes the alternative use of the same scarce resources.","difference":"Some opportunity costs are monetary; others are time, risk, flexibility, or foregone learning.","why_useful":"It makes hidden costs visible."},"common_mistakes":["Treating sunk costs as future opportunity costs.","Adding every rejected alternative together."],"takeaway":"Because resources are scarce, choosing one use means giving up the best alternative use.","challenge":"A city spends $20 million on a stadium instead of replacing water mains. Explain the opportunity cost without saying only “$20 million.”","quiz":{"prompt":"You spend Saturday repairing your house instead of taking a paid $300 shift, which was your best alternative. What is the clearest opportunity cost?","options":["The $300 earnings you gave up","Every activity you could possibly have done","Only the cost of repair materials","Zero because you did not spend $300"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Opportunity cost is the value of the best forgone alternative.","feedback":["Correct.","Only the best forgone alternative counts.","Materials are an explicit cost, but the question asks for the forgone alternative.","Foregone income is still a real economic cost."]}},"economics_money_and_banking":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Money & Banking: how deposits, loans, and payments connect","objective":"Explain the functions of money and how banks transform deposits into credit while managing liquidity and risk.","key_terms":[{"term":"Money","definition":"An asset widely accepted as a medium of exchange, unit of account, and store of value."},{"term":"Deposit","definition":"A bank liability owed to the customer."},{"term":"Loan","definition":"A bank asset representing money owed by a borrower."},{"term":"Liquidity","definition":"Ability to meet near-term cash and payment obligations."}],"explanation":"Money makes exchange easier because people do not need a perfect barter match. Banks sit inside the payment system and also connect savers with borrowers. On a bank balance sheet, customer deposits are liabilities because the bank owes that money to depositors; loans are assets because borrowers owe money to the bank.\n\nBanks do not simply place every deposited dollar in a vault. They hold liquid assets and reserves while using part of their funding to make loans and buy other assets. This creates maturity and liquidity risk: depositors may want money back quickly, while many loans are repaid slowly over years.","worked_example":{"setup":"A customer deposits $10,000 into a bank.","steps":["The bank records a $10,000 deposit liability to the customer.","The bank receives corresponding cash/reserve assets.","It may later use part of its available funding to make a qualified loan, creating a new loan asset.","The bank must still satisfy capital, liquidity, reserve/payment, and risk-management requirements."],"result":"Banking is balance-sheet transformation, not simply storage."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Money and banking connect directly to interest rates and monetary policy.","explanation":"Central-bank policy changes the price and availability of short-term funding, influencing bank lending and broader financial conditions.","difference":"Commercial banks decide whom to lend to; the central bank sets system-level monetary conditions.","why_useful":"It explains how policy rates reach households and businesses."},"common_mistakes":["Thinking a customer deposit is an asset from the bank’s perspective.","Assuming banks can lend without capital, liquidity, or credit constraints."],"takeaway":"Banks fund assets such as loans with liabilities such as deposits, while the payment and central-bank systems keep money transferable.","challenge":"Explain why a bank can be profitable but still face a liquidity problem.","quiz":{"prompt":"On a commercial bank’s balance sheet, a customer checking deposit is usually…","options":["A liability of the bank","An asset of the bank","Bank equity","Revenue"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"The bank owes the deposited funds to the customer, so the deposit is a liability.","feedback":["Correct.","The customer has the asset; the bank has the liability.","Deposits are not owners’ equity.","Receiving a deposit does not itself create revenue."]}},"economics_monetary_policy":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Monetary Policy: how central banks influence demand and inflation","objective":"Trace how a central-bank interest-rate change can affect borrowing, spending, asset prices, employment, and inflation.","key_terms":[{"term":"Policy rate","definition":"The short-term interest rate a central bank targets or administers."},{"term":"Tightening","definition":"Policy intended to restrain demand and inflation, commonly through higher rates."},{"term":"Easing","definition":"Policy intended to support demand, commonly through lower rates."}],"explanation":"Monetary policy works mainly by changing financial conditions. When a central bank raises its policy rate, short-term borrowing becomes more expensive and many other interest rates tend to rise. Mortgages, business loans, and other credit can become costlier. Some households and firms postpone spending or investment, so total demand cools.\n\nThe process is indirect and delayed. Exchange rates, asset prices, bank lending standards, expectations, and debt structures all affect how strongly a rate change reaches the economy.","worked_example":{"setup":"Inflation is running well above target and demand is strong.","steps":["The central bank raises its policy rate.","Market interest rates and borrowing costs rise.","Interest-sensitive purchases and investment slow.","Weaker demand reduces pressure on labor, production capacity, and prices over time."],"result":"Inflation pressure may fall, but the policy can also slow growth and employment."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Monetary and fiscal policy both influence aggregate demand, but through different institutions and tools.","explanation":"Fiscal policy changes government spending/taxes; monetary policy changes financial conditions and the cost of credit.","difference":"Central banks usually operate independently of the government budget.","why_useful":"Separating the two prevents labeling every economic intervention “the Fed” or “government spending.”"},"common_mistakes":["Expecting rate changes to affect inflation immediately.","Assuming higher rates lower every price; the goal is usually to slow the rate of overall price growth."],"takeaway":"Monetary policy changes financial conditions to influence total demand, with effects that arrive through multiple channels and lags.","challenge":"Trace a policy-rate increase through a homebuyer, a business investment decision, and aggregate demand.","quiz":{"prompt":"A central bank raises its policy rate substantially. What is the most typical near-term transmission mechanism?","options":["Borrowing becomes more expensive and some spending slows","Government purchases automatically rise","All wages fall immediately","Productivity instantly increases"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Higher policy rates generally tighten financial conditions and reduce interest-sensitive demand.","feedback":["Correct.","Government purchases are fiscal policy.","Wages do not mechanically fall immediately.","Interest-rate changes do not instantly raise productivity."]}},"economics_elasticity":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Elasticity: measuring how strongly buyers and sellers respond","objective":"Calculate and interpret price elasticity and use it to predict quantity and revenue responses to price changes.","key_terms":[{"term":"Price elasticity of demand","definition":"Percentage change in quantity demanded divided by percentage change in price."},{"term":"Elastic","definition":"Quantity responds proportionally more than price; absolute elasticity is greater than 1."},{"term":"Inelastic","definition":"Quantity responds proportionally less than price; absolute elasticity is less than 1."}],"explanation":"Elasticity adds a crucial question to supply and demand: not just which direction will quantity move, but by how much? Price elasticity of demand compares the percentage change in quantity demanded with the percentage change in price. Economists usually discuss the absolute value, because demand normally moves opposite price.\n\nIf a 10% price increase causes quantity demanded to fall 20%, the elasticity is about 2, so demand is elastic. If the same 10% price increase reduces quantity only 3%, elasticity is about 0.3, so demand is inelastic. Substitutes, time to adjust, necessity versus luxury, and the share of a buyer’s budget all influence elasticity.\n\nElasticity also helps predict total revenue. With elastic demand, a price increase can reduce revenue because quantity falls by a larger percentage than price rises. With inelastic demand, a price increase can raise revenue because quantity falls relatively little.","worked_example":{"setup":"A streaming service raises its monthly price from $10 to $11, a 10% increase, and subscriptions fall from 1,000 to 850, a 15% decrease.","steps":["Percentage price change ≈ +10%.","Percentage quantity change ≈ −15%.","Elasticity magnitude ≈ 15% / 10% = 1.5.","Because 1.5 > 1, demand is elastic around this price range.","Old revenue = $10 × 1,000 = $10,000; new revenue = $11 × 850 = $9,350."],"result":"The price rose, but revenue fell because quantity responded more strongly."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Elasticity describes the steepness of behavioral response behind supply-and-demand movements.","explanation":"Supply and demand tell you direction; elasticity tells you sensitivity.","difference":"Elasticity is not a fixed property forever. It can change by price range, time horizon, market definition, and available substitutes.","why_useful":"It lets you reason about taxes, pricing, revenue, and policy incidence quantitatively."},"common_mistakes":["Using dollar changes instead of percentage changes when comparing different scales.","Assuming every necessity is perfectly inelastic.","Treating elasticity as one permanent number for an entire market."],"takeaway":"Elasticity measures responsiveness; it turns directional supply-and-demand logic into a statement about magnitude.","challenge":"A medication has few substitutes in the short run. Predict whether demand is likely relatively elastic or inelastic and explain why.","quiz":{"prompt":"A 5% increase in price causes quantity demanded to fall 15%. Demand is best described as…","options":["Elastic","Inelastic","Perfectly inelastic","Unit elastic"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"The elasticity magnitude is 15% ÷ 5% = 3, which is greater than 1.","feedback":["Correct.","Inelastic would be less than 1.","Perfectly inelastic means quantity does not respond at all.","Unit elastic would equal 1."]}},"economics_market_structures":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Market Structures: competition, market power, and strategic behavior","objective":"Distinguish major market structures and explain how barriers to entry and the number of competitors affect pricing power.","key_terms":[{"term":"Market power","definition":"Ability of a seller to influence price rather than simply accept a market price."},{"term":"Barrier to entry","definition":"A cost, legal rule, technology, network, or other obstacle that makes entry difficult."},{"term":"Oligopoly","definition":"A market dominated by a small number of strategically interdependent firms."}],"explanation":"Market structure describes the competitive environment firms face. In a highly competitive market with many sellers and a standardized product, one firm has little power to raise price above the market level because buyers can switch easily. At the other extreme, a monopoly has no close competitor in the defined market and may have substantial pricing power, especially when entry is difficult.\n\nMonopolistic competition combines many sellers with differentiated products, so firms have some pricing power but still face significant competition. Oligopoly involves a small number of major firms, which creates strategic interdependence: one firm’s pricing, capacity, or product decisions can change what rivals do.\n\nThe labels are useful because they point to mechanisms—substitutability, entry, concentration, and strategic response—not because every industry fits perfectly into one box.","worked_example":{"setup":"Compare a wheat farmer with a patented-drug producer.","steps":["The individual wheat farmer sells a product very similar to many competitors’ output.","If the farmer demands a much higher price, buyers can switch to another seller.","A patented drug can have legal protection from close copies for a period of time.","With fewer substitutes and a barrier to entry, the drug producer has more ability to choose price above marginal cost."],"result":"Different competitive conditions create different amounts of market power."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Market structure changes how the supply side responds to price and competition.","explanation":"The basic supply-demand model often assumes price-taking firms; market-structure analysis relaxes that assumption.","difference":"Concentration alone does not prove harmful market power; contestability, substitutes, regulation, and technology also matter.","why_useful":"It explains why identical cost changes can produce different pricing behavior in different industries."},"common_mistakes":["Calling any large company a monopoly. The relevant issue is competition in a defined market.","Assuming oligopoly firms make decisions independently of rivals."],"takeaway":"Market power grows when buyers have fewer substitutes and rivals face meaningful barriers to entry.","challenge":"Explain why an industry with three major firms might behave differently from one with thousands of small firms even if both sell useful products.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which feature most directly supports persistent monopoly power?","options":["Strong barriers that prevent new competitors from entering","Many close substitutes","Thousands of equally sized sellers","Very low switching costs"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Barriers to entry help prevent high profits or prices from attracting effective new competition.","feedback":["Correct.","Close substitutes reduce market power.","Many sellers is characteristic of competition, not monopoly.","Low switching costs make it easier for buyers to leave."]}},"economics_externalities_and_public_goods":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Externalities & Public Goods: when private choices spill over to others","objective":"Explain why external costs, external benefits, and public-good characteristics can make unregulated market outcomes inefficient.","key_terms":[{"term":"Externality","definition":"A cost or benefit from an activity that affects someone not fully represented in the transaction."},{"term":"Public good","definition":"A good that is non-rival and difficult to exclude non-payers from using."},{"term":"Free-rider problem","definition":"People can benefit without paying, weakening private incentives to provide the good."}],"explanation":"Markets coordinate private benefits and private costs well when the decision-maker bears the relevant consequences. An externality appears when part of a cost or benefit falls on someone outside the transaction. Pollution is a negative externality when a factory and its customers do not bear the full health or environmental cost. Vaccination can create positive external benefits by reducing disease transmission to others.\n\nPublic goods create a related problem. A good is non-rival if one person’s use does not meaningfully reduce another person’s use, and non-excludable if it is difficult to prevent non-payers from benefiting. National defense is a classic example. Because people can free-ride, voluntary private payment may provide less than the socially desired amount.\n\nTaxes, subsidies, regulation, property rights, cap-and-trade systems, and public provision are different tools aimed at aligning private incentives with broader social costs and benefits.","worked_example":{"setup":"A factory earns profit from production but emits pollution that damages nearby property.","steps":["The factory considers wages, materials, energy, and other private production costs.","Nearby residents bear a pollution cost that may not appear on the factory’s books.","The private cost of one more unit is therefore lower than the full social cost.","The market can produce more of the polluting good than would be chosen if the external damage were priced."],"result":"A policy that makes the external cost part of the decision can move private incentives closer to social costs."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Externalities modify ordinary supply-and-demand analysis by adding costs or benefits that market prices may omit.","explanation":"The market curve reflects private incentives; social analysis asks whether third-party effects change the true marginal cost or benefit.","difference":"Not every unpleasant effect is an externality—if it is already fully priced into the transaction, it is internalized.","why_useful":"It gives a precise reason for some policy interventions instead of assuming markets always fail or governments always improve them."},"common_mistakes":["Calling any public service a public good. The technical test is non-rivalry and non-excludability.","Assuming the best response to every externality is to ban the activity entirely."],"takeaway":"Externalities and public goods matter because market prices can omit important social costs or benefits.","challenge":"Explain how a congestion charge can change a driver’s private decision when road use creates costs for other drivers.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which is the clearest negative externality?","options":["A factory’s emissions impose health costs on nearby residents not included in the product price","A customer pays a higher price for a luxury watch","A company pays wages to employees","A restaurant buys vegetables from a supplier"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"The health cost falls on third parties outside the direct buyer-seller transaction.","feedback":["Correct.","The buyer directly faces the price.","Wages are part of the transaction.","The supplier is directly compensated."]}},"economics_labor_markets":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Labor Markets: why wages and employment depend on both workers and firms","objective":"Explain labor demand, labor supply, productivity, human capital, and why wages differ across jobs and markets.","key_terms":[{"term":"Derived demand","definition":"Firms demand labor because workers help produce goods and services customers value."},{"term":"Human capital","definition":"Skills, knowledge, health, and experience that raise productive capability."},{"term":"Monopsony","definition":"A labor market where an employer or small group of employers has meaningful wage-setting power."}],"explanation":"Labor is bought and sold differently from ordinary products, but supply-and-demand logic still helps. Firms demand workers because workers contribute to output. The value of hiring an additional worker depends on the worker’s additional production and the value of that output. If technology or demand makes workers more productive or their output more valuable, labor demand can rise.\n\nWorkers supply labor based on wages, working conditions, location, schedules, benefits, family constraints, alternatives, and preferences. Training and experience can raise human capital, but wage differences also reflect risk, bargaining power, discrimination, licensing, unionization, geography, and employer market power.\n\nA competitive labor market is therefore a useful baseline, not a complete description of every workplace.","worked_example":{"setup":"A commercial HVAC contractor sees a surge in projects and qualified technicians are scarce.","steps":["More projects raise the value of additional technician hours to the contractor.","Labor demand for technicians shifts right.","If the local supply of qualified technicians cannot expand quickly, employers compete for a limited pool.","Wages, overtime, signing bonuses, or training investment may rise."],"result":"Wages and employment respond to both the value of workers’ output and the availability of workers with the needed skills."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Labor markets connect productivity to wages through the value workers add to production.","explanation":"Economic growth, education, technology, immigration, and business cycles can all change labor demand or supply.","difference":"A worker’s wage is not a pure measurement of personal worth or even productivity; institutions and bargaining conditions matter.","why_useful":"It prevents simplistic explanations of wage changes."},"common_mistakes":["Assuming wages are determined only by how hard a job feels.","Ignoring employer market power or barriers that limit worker mobility."],"takeaway":"Wages and employment emerge from the interaction of worker supply, employer demand, productivity, alternatives, and institutions.","challenge":"Explain why a shortage of licensed technicians can raise wages before the number of workers has time to increase.","quiz":{"prompt":"Why is labor demand called “derived demand”?","options":["Firms hire workers because workers help produce goods and services customers demand","Workers always demand higher wages","Labor demand is set directly by law","It is derived only from population size"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"The value of labor comes from its contribution to producing valuable output.","feedback":["Correct.","Workers’ wage preferences are labor supply, not why firms demand labor.","Law can influence labor markets but does not create the basic derived-demand mechanism.","Population can affect supply and demand but is not the definition."]}},"economics_international_trade":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"International Trade: why comparative advantage can create gains","objective":"Use opportunity cost and comparative advantage to explain gains from specialization while recognizing distributional effects and tradeoffs.","key_terms":[{"term":"Comparative advantage","definition":"Ability to produce a good at lower opportunity cost than another producer."},{"term":"Absolute advantage","definition":"Ability to produce more output with the same resources."},{"term":"Tariff","definition":"A tax on imports."}],"explanation":"The central logic of trade is comparative advantage, not simply who is best at producing everything. Two countries can both gain from specialization and exchange when their opportunity costs differ. Even if one country is more productive in both goods, it can still benefit by specializing more in the good where its relative advantage is greatest.\n\nTrade can lower prices, expand product variety, let firms reach larger markets, and move production toward lower-opportunity-cost uses. But gains are not distributed evenly. Workers, regions, or firms exposed to import competition can lose income or face costly transitions even when the country’s total gains are positive.\n\nTariffs can protect some domestic producers by raising import prices, but consumers and downstream businesses pay more, and trading partners can retaliate.","worked_example":{"setup":"Country A can produce either 10 machines or 20 tons of grain with a fixed resource bundle. Country B can produce either 4 machines or 12 tons of grain.","steps":["In A, 1 machine costs 2 tons of grain in opportunity cost.","In B, 1 machine costs 3 tons of grain.","A has comparative advantage in machines because it gives up less grain per machine.","For grain, A gives up 0.5 machine per ton while B gives up about 0.33 machine, so B has comparative advantage in grain.","Specialization and trade at an exchange rate between those opportunity costs can make both better off."],"result":"Comparative advantage is about relative opportunity cost, not absolute productivity."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Trade is opportunity-cost reasoning applied across producers and countries.","explanation":"The same scarcity logic used for personal choices determines which production patterns can create mutual gains.","difference":"Real trade also involves transportation, strategic industries, exchange rates, adjustment costs, politics, and national security.","why_useful":"It separates the core efficiency argument from the separate question of who wins and loses."},"common_mistakes":["Confusing absolute advantage with comparative advantage.","Assuming aggregate gains mean every worker or region gains."],"takeaway":"Trade can create gains when parties specialize according to comparative advantage, but those gains can be distributed unevenly.","challenge":"Explain why a highly productive country can still import a product it is capable of making efficiently.","quiz":{"prompt":"Comparative advantage is determined by…","options":["Lower opportunity cost","Higher total population","A trade surplus","Being absolutely best at producing every good"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Comparative advantage compares what must be given up to produce one more unit.","feedback":["Correct.","Population does not define comparative advantage.","A trade balance is an outcome, not the definition.","Absolute advantage is different from comparative advantage."]}},"economics_economic_growth":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Economic Growth: how productivity raises living standards over time","objective":"Explain how productivity, capital, human capital, technology, and institutions contribute to sustained growth in real output per person.","key_terms":[{"term":"Real GDP per capita","definition":"Inflation-adjusted output divided by population; a rough measure of average material production per person."},{"term":"Productivity","definition":"Output produced per unit of input, often measured as output per worker or hour."},{"term":"Capital deepening","definition":"Increasing the amount or quality of productive capital available per worker."}],"explanation":"Long-run improvements in material living standards depend heavily on productivity: producing more valuable output from each hour of work and each unit of resources. More and better physical capital—machines, tools, infrastructure, software—can raise worker output. Education, training, and health build human capital. Technology and organizational improvements let the same inputs produce more.\n\nSimply adding capital eventually faces diminishing returns if technology and skills do not improve. Sustained growth therefore depends strongly on innovation, institutions that support investment and exchange, reliable infrastructure, knowledge diffusion, and incentives to develop and adopt better methods.\n\nReal GDP per capita is useful but incomplete. It does not fully measure health, leisure, environmental quality, household production, inequality, or every aspect of well-being.","worked_example":{"setup":"A fabrication shop has ten workers making parts with old manual equipment.","steps":["The firm installs CNC equipment and trains workers to program it.","Each worker can now produce more precise parts per hour.","Higher output per worker lowers unit costs and can support higher wages, lower prices, or higher profits.","If similar productivity improvements spread across many industries, real output per person can grow."],"result":"Sustained growth comes from improvements in productive capability, not merely from working more hours forever."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Economic growth connects technology, education, capital investment, energy systems, and institutions through productivity.","explanation":"Each can change how much value an economy produces from available labor and resources.","difference":"Short-run booms in aggregate demand can raise output temporarily without permanently increasing long-run productive capacity.","why_useful":"It distinguishes cyclical recovery from genuine increases in long-run capacity."},"common_mistakes":["Treating nominal GDP growth caused by inflation as real economic growth.","Assuming more machines alone guarantee unlimited growth."],"takeaway":"Sustained growth in living standards requires rising productivity, supported by capital, skills, technology, and effective institutions.","challenge":"Explain why a one-time increase in consumer spending is different from a productivity improvement when thinking about 20-year growth.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which change most directly supports sustained growth in real GDP per person?","options":["A lasting rise in productivity","A one-time increase in the price level","Printing larger-denomination currency","A temporary holiday shopping surge"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Productivity raises the economy’s ability to produce real output per unit of input over time.","feedback":["Correct.","Inflation raises nominal values, not necessarily real output.","Currency denomination does not increase productive capacity.","A temporary demand surge is not sustained productivity growth."]}},"accounting_balance_sheet":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Balance Sheet: a snapshot of what a business owns and owes","objective":"Read a balance sheet using Assets = Liabilities + Equity and explain how common transactions affect both sides.","key_terms":[{"term":"Asset","definition":"A resource controlled by the business with expected future economic benefit."},{"term":"Liability","definition":"An obligation the business owes to others."},{"term":"Equity","definition":"The owners’ residual claim after liabilities are subtracted from assets."}],"explanation":"A balance sheet is a snapshot at one point in time. It lists assets on one side and the claims against those assets—liabilities and owners’ equity—on the other. The accounting equation must balance: Assets = Liabilities + Equity.\n\nThe equation balances because every transaction has at least two effects. Borrowing cash increases an asset (cash) and a liability (loan payable). An owner investing cash increases an asset and equity. Paying down debt reduces cash and reduces a liability.","worked_example":{"setup":"A new company starts when the owner contributes $20,000 cash and then the company borrows $30,000 from a bank.","steps":["Owner contribution: Cash +$20,000; Equity +$20,000.","Bank loan: Cash +$30,000; Loan payable +$30,000.","Total assets are now $50,000 cash.","Liabilities are $30,000 and equity is $20,000."],"result":"$50,000 Assets = $30,000 Liabilities + $20,000 Equity."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"The balance sheet connects to the income statement and cash-flow statement.","explanation":"Profit changes equity over time, while cash-flow activity explains why cash changed between two balance-sheet dates.","difference":"The balance sheet is a point-in-time snapshot; the income and cash-flow statements cover a period of time.","why_useful":"Together the statements explain financial position, performance, and cash movement."},"common_mistakes":["Treating revenue as an asset category.","Thinking a loan increases profit when cash is borrowed."],"takeaway":"Every balance-sheet transaction preserves Assets = Liabilities + Equity, even when the mix changes.","challenge":"A business buys a $12,000 truck by paying $2,000 cash and borrowing $10,000. Describe the balance-sheet changes.","quiz":{"prompt":"A company borrows $25,000 cash from a bank. What happens immediately?","options":["Assets rise $25,000 and liabilities rise $25,000","Revenue rises $25,000","Assets rise $25,000 and equity falls $25,000","Liabilities fall $25,000"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Cash increases and the new loan obligation increases by the same amount.","feedback":["Correct.","Borrowing is financing, not revenue.","Equity does not fall merely because debt is issued.","A new loan creates a liability."]}},"accounting_accounting_equation":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"The Accounting Equation: why the books must balance","objective":"Use Assets = Liabilities + Equity to analyze simple business transactions.","key_terms":[{"term":"Assets","definition":"Resources controlled by the business."},{"term":"Liabilities","definition":"Claims of creditors."},{"term":"Equity","definition":"Owners’ residual claim."}],"explanation":"The accounting equation is the structural rule behind double-entry accounting: Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Assets show what the business controls. Liabilities show what it owes creditors. Equity is what remains for owners after liabilities are subtracted.\n\nTransactions do not break the equation; they change two or more accounts in ways that keep both sides equal.","worked_example":{"setup":"An owner contributes $5,000 cash to start a company.","steps":["Cash, an asset, increases by $5,000.","Owner equity also increases by $5,000."],"result":"Assets +$5,000 = Equity +$5,000."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"The equation is the foundation of the balance sheet and debit/credit system.","explanation":"Debits and credits are the recording mechanics used to preserve this equation transaction by transaction.","difference":"The equation describes relationships; debit/credit rules describe how entries are recorded.","why_useful":"If an entry breaks the equation, something is missing or misclassified."},"common_mistakes":["Assuming equity means cash.","Treating every cash inflow as revenue."],"takeaway":"Every valid transaction keeps total assets equal to total liabilities plus equity.","challenge":"A company buys $1,000 of equipment for cash. Explain why total assets do not change.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which equation must a balance sheet satisfy?","options":["Assets = Liabilities + Equity","Revenue = Cash + Debt","Profit = Assets + Liabilities","Cash = Equity − Revenue"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"This is the fundamental accounting equation.","feedback":["Correct.","This is not an accounting identity.","Profit is not defined this way.","Cash is only one asset."]}},"biology_gene_expression":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Gene Expression: how DNA information becomes a functional product","objective":"Trace the flow from DNA to RNA to protein and explain how cells regulate which genes are active.","key_terms":[{"term":"Transcription","definition":"Making an RNA copy from a DNA template."},{"term":"mRNA","definition":"Messenger RNA that carries coding information from DNA."},{"term":"Translation","definition":"Using an mRNA sequence to build a polypeptide at a ribosome."},{"term":"Regulation","definition":"Control over when, where, and how strongly a gene is expressed."}],"explanation":"A gene is a DNA sequence that contains information for making a functional product, often a protein. For a protein-coding gene, expression usually begins with transcription: RNA polymerase uses one DNA strand as a template to build messenger RNA. In eukaryotic cells, that RNA is processed before leaving the nucleus.\n\nDuring translation, a ribosome reads the mRNA in three-base codons. Transfer RNAs bring amino acids whose anticodons match those codons, and the ribosome links the amino acids into a chain. The chain then folds and may be modified into a functional protein.\n\nCells do not express every gene equally. Regulatory proteins, DNA accessibility, signaling pathways, RNA stability, and other mechanisms determine which genes are turned on and how much product is made.","worked_example":{"setup":"A pancreatic beta cell needs to produce insulin.","steps":["Signals and regulatory proteins activate the insulin gene.","The DNA sequence is transcribed into RNA.","The RNA is processed into mature mRNA.","Ribosomes translate the mRNA into a protein precursor.","The protein is processed and packaged so insulin can be secreted."],"result":"Different cell types can contain the same DNA but behave differently because they express different sets of genes."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Gene expression connects genetics to cell function.","explanation":"DNA stores information, but expression determines when that information is converted into molecules that actually change cell behavior.","difference":"Having a gene does not mean it is active, and a gene’s DNA sequence is not itself the protein.","why_useful":"This explains how one genome can produce neurons, muscle cells, liver cells, and many other cell types."},"common_mistakes":["Saying DNA directly turns into protein. RNA is an essential intermediate for protein-coding genes.","Assuming all genes are active in every cell."],"takeaway":"For many genes, information flows DNA → RNA → protein, while regulation controls when and how strongly that flow occurs.","challenge":"Explain why a liver cell and a neuron can contain nearly the same DNA yet make very different proteins.","quiz":{"prompt":"What happens during translation?","options":["A ribosome reads mRNA to build a polypeptide","DNA is copied into DNA","RNA polymerase builds mRNA from DNA","A chromosome is duplicated before cell division"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Translation is the ribosome-mediated conversion of mRNA codons into an amino-acid sequence.","feedback":["Correct.","That is DNA replication.","That is transcription.","That describes chromosome replication, not translation."]}},"reasoning_claims_and_evidence":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Claims & Evidence: how to tell whether a conclusion is actually supported","objective":"Separate claims from evidence and evaluate whether the evidence is relevant, reliable, and strong enough for the conclusion.","key_terms":[{"term":"Claim","definition":"A statement asserted to be true."},{"term":"Evidence","definition":"Information that increases or decreases support for a claim."},{"term":"Warrant","definition":"The reasoning that connects the evidence to the claim."}],"explanation":"Good reasoning starts by separating what someone is claiming from what they are using to support it. Evidence is not strong merely because it is detailed or confidently stated. It should be relevant to the claim, come from a source and method appropriate to the question, and survive plausible alternative explanations.\n\nThe warrant is often the hidden step. If someone says “sales rose after the new manager arrived, therefore the manager caused the increase,” the evidence is the sales change and timing, but the warrant assumes no other important cause changed. Seasonality, pricing, a competitor closing, or a broader market boom could also explain the result.\n\nStrong reasoning therefore asks: What exactly is the claim? What observation would we expect if it were true? What else could produce the same observation? What evidence would distinguish those possibilities?","worked_example":{"setup":"A company says its new training program increased productivity because output rose 12% after training.","steps":["Claim: the training caused the productivity increase.","Evidence: output was 12% higher afterward.","Possible alternatives: new equipment, easier jobs, overtime, staffing changes, or higher demand.","Better evidence would compare trained and similar untrained workers or control for those other changes."],"result":"The original evidence is relevant but not yet enough to isolate causation."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Claims-and-evidence reasoning is the foundation for science, statistics, history, medicine, and media literacy.","explanation":"Every field asks whether observations justify a conclusion and what competing explanation remains.","difference":"Different fields use different evidence standards; a controlled experiment, archival document, engineering test, and legal record are not interchangeable.","why_useful":"It gives you a repeatable method for evaluating arguments across domains."},"common_mistakes":["Treating an example as proof of a general rule.","Judging a claim by whether you like the conclusion instead of the evidence."],"takeaway":"Evidence supports a claim only through a defensible reasoning link and after serious alternative explanations are considered.","challenge":"Take the claim “remote work makes employees more productive.” Name one useful piece of evidence and one alternative explanation it would need to address.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which question best tests the strength of evidence for a causal claim?","options":["What other plausible causes could produce the same result?","Do I agree with the conclusion?","Is the claim popular?","Was the evidence presented confidently?"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Competing explanations are central to judging whether evidence isolates the claimed cause.","feedback":["Correct.","Agreement is not evidence quality.","Popularity does not establish truth.","Confidence of presentation does not make evidence causal."]}},"reasoning_correlation_vs_causation":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":8,"title":"Correlation vs Causation: why moving together does not prove one thing caused the other","objective":"Identify confounding and reverse causality and explain why experiments or stronger designs improve causal inference.","key_terms":[{"term":"Correlation","definition":"Two variables vary together statistically."},{"term":"Causation","definition":"Changing one factor produces a change in another, under the relevant conditions."},{"term":"Confounder","definition":"A third factor related to both variables that can create or distort an observed relationship."},{"term":"Reverse causality","definition":"The supposed outcome actually helps cause the supposed cause."}],"explanation":"Correlation is a pattern: when one variable changes, another tends to change too. That pattern is useful, but it does not by itself identify the direction or mechanism of cause. A third variable can affect both, the direction of causation can run backward, or the association can arise from selection or chance.\n\nRandomized experiments strengthen causal inference because random assignment tends to balance both known and unknown confounding factors across groups. When experiments are impossible, researchers use natural experiments, longitudinal data, controls, instrumental variables, difference-in-differences, and other designs to make alternative explanations less plausible.\n\nCausal reasoning is therefore not “correlation means nothing.” Correlation is evidence that can motivate investigation. The question is what additional design or evidence is needed before calling the relationship causal.","worked_example":{"setup":"Cities with more firefighters at a fire scene often have more property damage.","steps":["Observed correlation: more firefighters is associated with more damage.","Naive causal story: firefighters cause damage.","Confounder: larger fires both cause more damage and require more firefighters.","Comparing fires of similar initial severity or using other causal methods would test the firefighter effect more fairly."],"result":"The correlation is real but its naive causal interpretation is wrong."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"This concept extends Claims & Evidence by focusing specifically on causal claims.","explanation":"A causal claim needs evidence that addresses alternative causes, especially confounding and reverse direction.","difference":"Some correlations are genuinely causal; the problem is that correlation alone cannot tell you which ones are.","why_useful":"It protects decision-making from acting on relationships that disappear when the true cause is identified."},"common_mistakes":["Saying “correlation does not imply causation” as if correlations are useless.","Controlling for variables blindly without considering whether they are causes, effects, or colliders."],"takeaway":"Correlation shows variables move together; causal inference requires evidence that rules out credible alternative explanations.","challenge":"Ice-cream sales and drownings both rise in summer. Identify a likely confounder and explain the causal structure.","quiz":{"prompt":"A study finds people who carry lighters have higher lung-cancer rates. What is the most obvious confounder?","options":["Smoking","Weather","Height","Blood type"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Smoking causes people to carry lighters and also increases lung-cancer risk.","feedback":["Correct.","Weather is much less directly related to both variables.","Height does not explain the association.","Blood type does not plausibly create this relationship."]}},"chemistry_states_of_matter":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"States of Matter: particles, energy, and phase changes","objective":"Explain solids, liquids, gases, and phase changes using particle motion, intermolecular attractions, temperature, and pressure.","key_terms":[{"term":"Solid","definition":"Particles remain near fixed positions and mainly vibrate."},{"term":"Liquid","definition":"Particles remain close together but can move past one another."},{"term":"Gas","definition":"Particles are widely separated and move freely through the container."},{"term":"Phase change","definition":"A physical transition between states, such as melting or vaporization."}],"explanation":"The familiar states of matter differ because particles have different amounts of freedom of motion relative to the attractions between them. In a solid, attractions hold particles in an organized or constrained arrangement. They still move, but mostly by vibrating around positions. In a liquid, particles remain close because attractions still matter, yet they have enough energy to slide past one another. That is why a liquid keeps roughly the same volume but takes the shape of its container. In a gas, particle kinetic energy is large enough relative to attractions that particles spend most of their time far apart, so a gas expands to fill its container.\n\nTemperature measures the average kinetic energy associated with particle motion. Heating a substance usually increases particle motion. During a phase change, however, added energy can go into separating particles rather than raising temperature. That is why boiling water can remain near its boiling temperature while heat continues to enter: the energy is being used to overcome intermolecular attractions and convert liquid into vapor.\n\nPressure matters most for gases because gas particles are far apart and compressible. Increasing pressure can favor a denser phase. The exact phase present therefore depends on both temperature and pressure.","worked_example":{"setup":"Imagine heating ice at standard atmospheric pressure.","steps":["Below 0°C, added heat raises the temperature of the solid and its particles vibrate more strongly.","At the melting point, added energy primarily loosens the structure holding molecules in the solid.","Once all ice has melted, additional heat raises the liquid water temperature.","At the boiling point, added energy separates molecules into the gas phase rather than immediately increasing temperature."],"result":"The temperature-vs-heat graph has sloped regions within a phase and flatter regions during idealized phase changes."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Particle energy versus attractive forces","explanation":"The same competition explains melting, evaporation, condensation, boiling, and freezing. Heating tends to favor states with greater particle freedom; cooling favors states where attractions organize particles more strongly.","difference":"Real substances can have complex crystal structures, multiple solid phases, or unusual phase behavior, so “solid/liquid/gas” is a useful framework rather than the whole story.","why_useful":"This particle model prepares you for thermochemistry because heat transfer changes particle energy and can drive phase changes."},"common_mistakes":["Thinking particles stop moving in a solid.","Assuming temperature must rise whenever heat is added.","Confusing evaporation, which can occur at a surface below the boiling point, with boiling throughout a liquid."],"takeaway":"States of matter reflect the balance between particle kinetic energy, intermolecular attractions, temperature, and pressure.","challenge":"Why does sweating cool skin? Use evaporation and particle energy to explain why the molecules that escape tend to carry away above-average kinetic energy.","quiz":{"prompt":"During boiling at constant pressure, heat continues entering a pure liquid while its temperature stays nearly constant. Where does much of that energy go?","options":["Into overcoming intermolecular attractions during vaporization","Into making every molecule heavier","Into removing all particle motion","Into changing the substance into a different element"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"During the phase change, added energy increases potential energy by separating particles rather than immediately increasing average kinetic energy.","feedback":["Correct.","Molecular mass does not increase during boiling.","Particles remain in motion.","Boiling is a physical change, not a change of element."]}},"chemistry_thermochemistry":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Thermochemistry: tracking energy in chemical change","objective":"Use system/surroundings, heat, enthalpy, and bond-energy ideas to determine whether a process releases or absorbs energy.","key_terms":[{"term":"System","definition":"The part of the universe being studied, such as the reacting chemicals."},{"term":"Surroundings","definition":"Everything outside the system that can exchange energy with it."},{"term":"Exothermic","definition":"A process that transfers heat from the system to the surroundings; ΔH is negative at constant pressure."},{"term":"Endothermic","definition":"A process that absorbs heat from the surroundings; ΔH is positive at constant pressure."}],"explanation":"Thermochemistry follows energy as physical and chemical processes occur. The first step is to define the system. If a reaction mixture is the system, the cup, air, and your hand are part of the surroundings. Energy is conserved, so energy leaving the system enters the surroundings and vice versa.\n\nAt constant pressure, chemists often track heat with the enthalpy change, ΔH. When a reaction releases heat, the products end at lower enthalpy than the reactants and ΔH is negative: the reaction is exothermic. When a reaction absorbs heat, the products are at higher enthalpy and ΔH is positive: the reaction is endothermic.\n\nChemical reactions require breaking some bonds and forming others. Breaking bonds requires energy; forming bonds releases energy. A reaction is exothermic overall when the energy released by forming product bonds exceeds the energy required to break reactant bonds. This is why “breaking bonds releases energy” is backwards—bond breaking costs energy.\n\nCalorimetry turns temperature change into an energy estimate. For a substance with mass m and specific heat c, q = mcΔT. If water in a calorimeter warms, the water gained energy, which usually means the reaction system released approximately that amount of heat, with the opposite sign.","worked_example":{"setup":"A reaction warms 100 g of water from 22°C to 27°C. Use c = 4.18 J/(g·°C).","steps":["Calculate ΔT = 27 - 22 = 5°C.","Compute heat gained by water: q = mcΔT = 100 × 4.18 × 5 = 2,090 J.","The surroundings (water) gained +2.09 kJ.","If heat loss elsewhere is negligible, the reaction system released about 2.09 kJ, so q_system ≈ -2.09 kJ."],"result":"The sign flips because energy gained by the surroundings was lost by the system."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Energy conservation links temperature change to reaction energy","explanation":"Thermochemistry takes the particle-energy ideas from states of matter and applies the same accounting to chemical reactions: energy can move between microscopic bonding arrangements and macroscopic heat in the surroundings.","difference":"Enthalpy is not identical to “temperature,” and spontaneous reactions are not always exothermic; entropy also matters for spontaneity.","why_useful":"This lets you interpret fuel combustion, cold packs, hot packs, metabolism, phase changes, and industrial energy balances with one conservation framework."},"common_mistakes":["Saying bond breaking releases energy. It requires energy.","Losing track of the sign: if the surroundings warm, the system generally lost heat.","Using Celsius temperature itself instead of the temperature change ΔT in q = mcΔT."],"takeaway":"Thermochemistry is energy bookkeeping: define the system, track the direction of heat flow, and conserve energy between system and surroundings.","challenge":"A disposable cold pack becomes cold when activated. Is the process inside the pack exothermic or endothermic relative to the pack system? Explain the direction of energy flow.","quiz":{"prompt":"A reaction causes the surrounding water to warm by 8°C. What is the best conclusion about the reaction system, assuming little heat escapes elsewhere?","options":["It released heat to the water","It absorbed heat from the water","Its atoms lost mass","No energy transfer occurred"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"If the water warmed, it gained heat; by conservation of energy, the reaction system supplied that heat and was exothermic.","feedback":["Correct.","That would tend to cool the water instead.","Ordinary chemical energy transfer does not require a measurable loss of atomic mass.","A temperature rise is evidence of energy transfer."]}},"ai_machine_learning_basics":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Machine Learning Basics: learning a mapping from examples","objective":"Distinguish rules-based programming from machine learning and explain features, targets, training, prediction, and generalization.","key_terms":[{"term":"Model","definition":"A mathematical function or system that maps inputs to outputs."},{"term":"Feature","definition":"An input variable used by a model."},{"term":"Target","definition":"The output a supervised model is trained to predict."},{"term":"Training","definition":"Adjusting model parameters using data so predictions better match an objective."}],"explanation":"Traditional programming often begins with explicit rules written by a human: if condition A occurs, perform action B. Machine learning is useful when the rule is difficult to write directly but examples are available. A model receives inputs—features—and produces an output such as a category, probability, number, or generated sequence. During training, an algorithm adjusts internal parameters so the model performs better on training examples according to a loss or objective.\n\nThe key challenge is generalization. Memorizing the training examples is not enough; the model should perform well on new examples drawn from the problem it is meant to solve. That is why data is commonly separated into training data and held-out validation or test data.\n\nA model can still fail even with high test accuracy if the data does not represent the real deployment environment. A hiring model trained on historically biased decisions can learn those patterns. A vision model trained in daylight may fail at night. Machine learning therefore combines statistics, data quality, optimization, evaluation, and domain knowledge—not simply “giving a computer data.”","worked_example":{"setup":"Suppose you want to predict whether an email is spam.","steps":["Features might include words in the message, sender information, links, and formatting patterns.","Training examples contain emails paired with known spam/not-spam labels.","The learning algorithm adjusts parameters to reduce prediction error on the training set.","A held-out test set estimates how the model performs on unseen emails.","After deployment, changing spam tactics can create data drift, so performance must be monitored."],"result":"The model learns statistical patterns that approximate a useful decision rule; it does not need a human to hand-code every spam phrase."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Learning from evidence rather than fixed rules","explanation":"Machine learning connects to statistics because both infer patterns from samples while managing uncertainty and generalization.","difference":"A learned correlation is not automatically causal or reliable outside the data distribution used for training.","why_useful":"This foundation makes later topics—neural networks, transformers, overfitting, evaluation, and AI agents—much easier to reason about."},"common_mistakes":["Assuming training accuracy equals real-world performance.","Treating the model as if it “understands” every feature the way a human does.","Ignoring whether training data represents deployment data."],"takeaway":"Machine learning fits a model to examples, but usefulness depends on generalizing to new, representative situations.","challenge":"Think of a task at work or home where the rules are hard to specify but many labeled examples could exist. Identify possible features, a target, and one way the data could mislead the model.","quiz":{"prompt":"Why do practitioners keep a test set separate from the data used to fit a model?","options":["To estimate performance on unseen data","To make the training set larger","To guarantee the model is unbiased","To remove the need for monitoring after deployment"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A held-out test set provides evidence about generalization because the model was not optimized directly on those examples.","feedback":["Correct.","Holding data out makes the training set smaller, not larger.","A test set does not guarantee fairness or lack of bias.","Deployment conditions can change, so monitoring is still needed."]}},"ai_training_vs_inference":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Training vs Inference: building a model versus using it","objective":"Explain the difference between training and inference, including why their computing, data, and failure modes differ.","key_terms":[{"term":"Training","definition":"Optimizing model parameters using examples and an objective function."},{"term":"Inference","definition":"Using fixed learned parameters to produce outputs for new inputs."},{"term":"Parameter","definition":"A learned numerical value inside a model."},{"term":"Loss","definition":"A numerical measure of how far model outputs are from the training objective."}],"explanation":"Training is the learning phase. The model processes examples, measures error with a loss function, and an optimizer changes the model’s parameters to reduce that loss. Large modern models can require many accelerators, enormous datasets, and long training runs because parameter updates must be calculated repeatedly.\n\nInference is the use phase. The learned parameters are normally held fixed while the system processes a new prompt, image, transaction, sensor reading, or other input and computes an output. Inference can still be computationally expensive, especially for large generative models, but it is different from retraining the model.\n\nThis distinction matters operationally. Training failures involve data quality, objectives, optimization, and overfitting. Inference failures include latency, hardware capacity, distribution shift, prompt/input problems, and model limitations. A deployed application can also retrieve information or call tools during inference without changing the base model’s learned weights.","worked_example":{"setup":"Consider a model that recognizes defective parts from camera images.","steps":["During training, thousands of labeled images are processed and parameters are updated to reduce classification loss.","Once deployed on the factory line, each new image is passed through the fixed model.","The resulting defect probability is an inference output.","If the factory changes lighting and accuracy drops, the team may adjust the deployment setup or collect new data and retrain later."],"result":"Training changes learned parameters; ordinary inference uses them."},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"The distinction separates learning from execution","explanation":"It connects AI to ordinary software operations: building a capability and serving that capability are separate workloads with different costs and monitoring needs.","difference":"Some systems perform online learning or fine-tuning, where parameters continue changing after deployment, so the boundary can be intentionally blurred.","why_useful":"Knowing which phase you are in prevents mistakes such as assuming every chatbot conversation retrains the underlying model."},"common_mistakes":["Thinking a model normally rewrites its weights after every user prompt.","Assuming inference is free because no training occurs.","Confusing retrieval of new information with training new model parameters."],"takeaway":"Training updates model parameters; inference applies the learned model to new inputs.","challenge":"A chatbot searches a database before answering a question. Does that database lookup automatically mean the language model was retrained? Explain.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes ordinary model inference?","options":["The model uses learned parameters to compute an output for a new input","The model repeatedly updates all parameters using a training dataset","The model deletes its learned weights after every request","The model guarantees the output is correct"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Inference applies learned parameters; it does not ordinarily perform the optimization process used in training.","feedback":["Correct.","That describes training.","Model weights persist.","Inference can produce errors."]}},"biology_cell_structure":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"explanation","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Cell Structure: how compartments divide the work of life","objective":"Identify major cell structures and explain how membranes, organelles, and molecular machinery cooperate to maintain a living cell.","key_terms":[{"term":"Cell membrane","definition":"A selectively permeable phospholipid bilayer controlling exchange and signaling."},{"term":"Nucleus","definition":"A membrane-bound compartment in eukaryotes that contains most genomic DNA."},{"term":"Ribosome","definition":"A molecular machine that translates mRNA into protein."},{"term":"Mitochondrion","definition":"An organelle that uses electrochemical gradients to support ATP production in eukaryotic cells."}],"explanation":"A cell is not a bag of chemicals. Its structure organizes reactions in space. The cell membrane forms a boundary made largely of phospholipids with embedded proteins. Because the membrane is selectively permeable, the cell can maintain concentrations of ions and molecules that differ from the environment—essential for signaling, energy use, and homeostasis.\n\nEukaryotic cells contain membrane-bound organelles. The nucleus houses most DNA and separates transcription from many cytoplasmic processes. Ribosomes read messenger RNA and assemble proteins. The endoplasmic reticulum helps synthesize and process proteins and lipids; the Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and ships many cellular products. Mitochondria use electron transport and proton gradients to generate much of the ATP used by many eukaryotic cells. Lysosomes and related compartments help break down and recycle material.\n\nBacteria and archaea lack a membrane-bound nucleus, but they are highly organized cells with membranes, ribosomes, DNA, and specialized molecular systems. The important idea is that cell structures exist because different jobs require controlled locations, gradients, transport, and molecular machinery.","worked_example":{"setup":"A pancreatic cell is producing a protein hormone for export.","steps":["DNA in the nucleus is transcribed into RNA.","Ribosomes associated with rough ER synthesize the protein into the secretory pathway.","The ER and Golgi modify and sort the protein.","Transport vesicles carry the product toward the cell membrane.","Vesicle and plasma membranes fuse, releasing the product outside the cell."],"result":"The path shows how organelles cooperate rather than acting as isolated “parts.”"},"connection":{"shared_mechanism":"Compartmentalization controls flows of matter and information","explanation":"Cell structure connects to engineering systems: boundaries, specialized subsystems, transport networks, energy supplies, and feedback all allow a complex system to work reliably.","difference":"Cells are not designed machines and biological components are dynamic, stochastic, and products of evolution, so engineering analogies have limits.","why_useful":"Understanding compartments makes metabolism, genetics, signaling, infection, and human physiology much easier to understand."},"common_mistakes":["Calling every cell structure an organelle. Ribosomes are structures but not membrane-bound organelles.","Assuming only animal cells have mitochondria; plants do too.","Thinking bacterial cells are unorganized because they lack a nucleus."],"takeaway":"Cell structures organize information, energy, synthesis, transport, and waste handling into controlled spaces.","challenge":"Trace a protein destined for secretion from genetic information to release outside a eukaryotic cell. Name at least three structures involved.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which structure directly reads mRNA to assemble a protein?","options":["Ribosome","Nucleus","Lysosome","Cell membrane"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Ribosomes translate the codon sequence in mRNA into an amino-acid chain.","feedback":["Correct.","The nucleus stores DNA and supports transcription but does not directly perform translation.","Lysosomes mainly break down material.","The membrane controls exchange but does not translate mRNA."]}},"mathematics_arithmetic_fluency":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Arithmetic Fluency: understanding the operations, not racing the clock","objective":"Choose and carry out basic arithmetic operations while keeping track of what the numbers mean.","key_terms":[{"term":"Addition","definition":"Combining quantities."},{"term":"Subtraction","definition":"Finding a difference or what remains."},{"term":"Multiplication","definition":"Equal groups or scaling."},{"term":"Division","definition":"Sharing or measuring how many groups fit."}],"explanation":"Arithmetic becomes useful when the operation matches the situation. Addition combines quantities: if you have 8 feet of duct and add 5 more, the total is 13 feet. Subtraction compares or removes: if a 20-foot run uses 13 feet, 7 feet remain.\n\nMultiplication is repeated equal grouping or scaling. Four filters at $9 each cost 4 × 9 = $36. Division reverses that relationship. If $36 buys four identical filters, each costs 36 ÷ 4 = $9.\n\nFluency does not mean guessing quickly. It means seeing the structure well enough to choose an operation, estimate whether the result is sensible, and calculate accurately. A useful habit is to estimate first. If 19 × 6 is a little less than 20 × 6 = 120, an answer such as 1,140 should immediately look wrong.","worked_example":{"setup":"A technician needs 6 identical pieces of insulation, each 18 inches long.","steps":["Estimate first: 6 × 20 is about 120 inches.","Calculate 6 × 18 = 108 inches.","Check: 108 is reasonably close to the 120-inch estimate."],"result":"The required total length is 108 inches."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Choose and carry out basic arithmetic operations while keeping track of what the numbers mean.","challenge":"Choose a real purchase or measurement from your day and explain why it calls for addition, subtraction, multiplication, or division.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Five boxes contain 12 items each. Which operation directly finds the total number of items?","options":["12 − 5","12 ÷ 5","5 × 12","5 + 12"],"correct_index":2,"rationale":"Equal groups are modeled by multiplication: 5 × 12 = 60.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Equal groups are modeled by multiplication: 5 × 12 = 60.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"statistics_probability_foundations":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Probability Foundations: measuring uncertainty","objective":"Interpret probability as a measure of how likely an outcome is, from impossible to certain.","key_terms":[{"term":"Outcome","definition":"A possible result."},{"term":"Probability","definition":"A number describing likelihood."},{"term":"Sample space","definition":"The set of possible outcomes."}],"explanation":"Probability gives us a disciplined way to talk about uncertainty. A probability of 0 means an event is impossible under the model; a probability of 1 means it is certain. A probability of 0.5 means the event has a 50% chance under the stated assumptions.\n\nFor equally likely simple outcomes, probability can often be found by counting: favorable outcomes divided by all possible outcomes. A fair six-sided die has six equally likely faces, so the probability of rolling a 4 is 1/6.\n\nThe assumptions matter. If a die is loaded, the faces are not equally likely and simple counting is no longer enough. Probability describes uncertainty under a model; it does not promise what will happen on one particular trial. A 70% chance of rain can still be followed by a dry day.","worked_example":{"setup":"A fair bag contains 3 blue marbles and 1 red marble.","steps":["There are 4 equally likely marbles.","Three of the four are blue.","Probability of blue = 3/4 = 0.75."],"result":"A random draw has a 75% chance of being blue."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Interpret probability as a measure of how likely an outcome is, from impossible to certain.","challenge":"Create a four-outcome situation and calculate the probability of one event.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"A fair coin is flipped once. What is the probability of heads?","options":["0","1/4","1/2","1"],"correct_index":2,"rationale":"A fair coin has two equally likely outcomes, so heads has probability 1/2.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","A fair coin has two equally likely outcomes, so heads has probability 1/2.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"health_wellness_sleep_and_circadian_health":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Sleep & Circadian Health: why timing and regularity matter","objective":"Explain how sleep pressure and the circadian clock interact to shape alertness and sleep.","key_terms":[{"term":"Circadian rhythm","definition":"An approximately 24-hour biological timing system."},{"term":"Sleep pressure","definition":"The drive to sleep that generally builds with time awake."},{"term":"Light cue","definition":"Environmental light that helps set circadian timing."}],"explanation":"Sleep is controlled by more than being tired. Two major processes interact. Sleep pressure tends to build the longer you are awake and decrease while you sleep. At the same time, the circadian system changes the body's tendency toward sleep and wakefulness across the day.\n\nLight is one of the strongest environmental signals for the circadian clock. Bright light late at night can shift sleep timing later, while morning light often helps anchor an earlier schedule. Regular sleep and wake times make these systems easier to align.\n\nGood sleep supports attention, memory, mood, metabolic regulation, immune function, and physical recovery. It does not guarantee health, and individual sleep needs differ. The useful principle is consistency plus enough sleep opportunity, not chasing a single perfect number.","worked_example":{"setup":"Someone sleeps 7.5 hours every night but moves bedtime between 9 p.m. and 2 a.m.","steps":["Total sleep duration may be adequate.","The large timing swings can make circadian alignment harder.","A more regular schedule can make sleep and wake timing more predictable."],"result":"Sleep amount and sleep timing both matter."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain how sleep pressure and the circadian clock interact to shape alertness and sleep.","challenge":"Name one part of your sleep schedule you could make more consistent and explain why it may help.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which signal most directly helps set the human circadian clock?","options":["Light exposure","Shoe size","Handedness","Blood type"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Light exposure is a major cue used by the circadian timing system.","feedback":["Light exposure is a major cue used by the circadian timing system.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"physical_health_movement_fundamentals":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Movement Fundamentals: control before load","objective":"Recognize the basic roles of joint motion, stability, force, and progressive practice in physical movement.","key_terms":[{"term":"Mobility","definition":"Available movement at a joint or region."},{"term":"Stability","definition":"Ability to control position and motion."},{"term":"Load","definition":"External or internal force the body must manage."}],"explanation":"Human movement is a coordinated system. Joints need enough mobility for the task, muscles need to create force, and the nervous system has to control the motion. A limitation in one area can change how another area moves.\n\nGood technique is task-specific. A squat, a stair climb, and lifting a box all involve different demands, but each benefits from controlled positions, balanced force, and a load appropriate to current capacity.\n\nProgress usually works better when demand increases gradually. Adding weight, repetitions, range of motion, speed, or complexity too quickly can outpace recovery and control. The goal is not perfect-looking movement; it is a movement strategy that is controlled, tolerable, and appropriate for the person and task.","worked_example":{"setup":"A person can squat comfortably with body weight but loses control when holding a heavy box.","steps":["The basic movement exists.","The added load exceeds current control or strength.","Reduce load, practice, then increase demand gradually."],"result":"The problem is not simply 'bad form'; capacity changed when load changed."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Recognize the basic roles of joint motion, stability, force, and progressive practice in physical movement.","challenge":"Choose one movement you do at work or home and identify its mobility, stability, and load demands.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What is usually the safest way to progress a new movement skill?","options":["Increase every variable at once","Gradually increase demand as control and capacity improve","Avoid recovery","Use pain as the main target"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Progressive demand lets the body adapt while preserving control.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Progressive demand lets the body adapt while preserving control.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"medicine_anatomy_foundations":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Anatomy Foundations: learning the body's map","objective":"Use anatomical position, directional terms, and body organization to describe where structures are.","key_terms":[{"term":"Anatomical position","definition":"Standard reference posture used to describe the body."},{"term":"Superior","definition":"Toward the head."},{"term":"Inferior","definition":"Toward the feet."},{"term":"Medial","definition":"Toward the body's midline."},{"term":"Lateral","definition":"Away from the midline."}],"explanation":"Anatomy begins with a shared map. Without standard reference terms, 'left,' 'above,' or 'inside' can become ambiguous. Anatomical position gives everyone the same starting point: standing upright, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward.\n\nDirectional terms describe relationships. The chest is superior to the abdomen. The ears are lateral to the nose. The heart is medial to the lungs. These terms are relative: a structure can be superior to one thing and inferior to another.\n\nThe body is also organized into levels—cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs cooperate in organ systems. Anatomy describes structure; physiology asks how those structures function. Keeping those two questions separate helps organize later medical learning.","worked_example":{"setup":"Describe the elbow relative to the wrist and shoulder.","steps":["The elbow is proximal to the wrist.","The elbow is distal to the shoulder.","Both statements use the limb's point of attachment as reference."],"result":"Anatomical terms make location precise without depending on viewpoint."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Use anatomical position, directional terms, and body organization to describe where structures are.","challenge":"Describe the location of one body structure using two anatomical directional terms.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which structure is lateral to the nose?","options":["Sternum","Ear","Navel","Spine"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"The ears lie farther from the body's midline than the nose.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","The ears lie farther from the body's midline than the nose.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"culture_society_norms_and_values":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Norms & Values: how groups shape expected behavior","objective":"Distinguish shared values from social norms and explain how they influence behavior.","key_terms":[{"term":"Value","definition":"A shared idea about what is important or desirable."},{"term":"Norm","definition":"An expectation about behavior."},{"term":"Sanction","definition":"A social response that rewards or discourages behavior."}],"explanation":"Values are broad ideas about what a group considers important—such as independence, loyalty, fairness, privacy, or respect for elders. Norms are more specific expectations about how people should behave in situations.\n\nA value can support many norms. If a workplace values safety, norms may include wearing protective equipment, reporting hazards, and stopping work when conditions are unsafe. People learn norms through families, schools, workplaces, media, institutions, and everyday interaction.\n\nNorms are not identical to laws. Some are formally enforced, but many are maintained through approval, disapproval, reputation, or habit. They can also conflict and change. Different groups within the same society may hold different norms, so culture should not be treated as one uniform rulebook.","worked_example":{"setup":"A company says safety is a core value and expects workers to report near misses.","steps":["Safety is the broad value.","Reporting near misses is a behavioral norm.","Praise or discipline can act as sanctions that reinforce the norm."],"result":"The value is the principle; the norm is the expected action."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Distinguish shared values from social norms and explain how they influence behavior.","challenge":"Give one example of a value and a related norm from a workplace, family, team, or community.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which best describes a social norm?","options":["A shared expectation about behavior","A gene","A weather pattern","A mathematical constant"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A norm is an expected pattern of behavior within a social setting.","feedback":["A norm is an expected pattern of behavior within a social setting.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"physics_units_and_measurement":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Units & Measurement: making numbers mean something","objective":"Use units, conversions, significant measurement, and dimensional checks to interpret physical quantities.","key_terms":[{"term":"Quantity","definition":"A measurable property such as length, time, or temperature."},{"term":"Unit","definition":"A standard used to express a quantity."},{"term":"Dimension","definition":"The physical type of a quantity, such as length or time."}],"explanation":"A number without a unit is often incomplete. Saying a duct is '12' does not tell you whether it is 12 inches, feet, or millimeters. Units connect numbers to physical meaning.\n\nConversions change the numerical representation without changing the physical quantity. Twelve inches and one foot describe the same length. A reliable conversion multiplies by a ratio equal to one, such as 1 ft / 12 in, so unwanted units cancel.\n\nUnits also catch mistakes. Speed has dimensions of length divided by time. If a calculation intended to produce speed ends in square feet, something went wrong. This is called dimensional analysis, and it is one of the simplest error-detection tools in science and engineering.","worked_example":{"setup":"A pipe is 72 inches long. Convert it to feet.","steps":["Use 1 foot = 12 inches.","72 in × (1 ft / 12 in) cancels inches.","72 ÷ 12 = 6."],"result":"The pipe is 6 feet long."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Use units, conversions, significant measurement, and dimensional checks to interpret physical quantities.","challenge":"Take one measurement you use in life or work and show a unit conversion with units canceling.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which result has units of speed?","options":["12 meters","12 seconds","12 meters per second","12 square meters"],"correct_index":2,"rationale":"Speed is distance divided by time, so meters per second has the correct dimensions.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Speed is distance divided by time, so meters per second has the correct dimensions.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"chemistry_atomic_structure":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Atomic Structure: what makes one element different from another","objective":"Explain the roles of protons, neutrons, and electrons and why proton number defines the element.","key_terms":[{"term":"Proton","definition":"Positively charged particle in the nucleus."},{"term":"Neutron","definition":"Electrically neutral particle in the nucleus."},{"term":"Electron","definition":"Negatively charged particle associated with the atom."},{"term":"Atomic number","definition":"Number of protons in the nucleus."}],"explanation":"Atoms contain a tiny, dense nucleus with protons and neutrons, surrounded by electrons described by quantum states. The proton count is the atom's atomic number, and that count defines the element.\n\nChanging the number of electrons changes electrical charge, producing an ion, but it does not change which element the atom is. Changing the number of neutrons produces a different isotope of the same element. Changing proton number creates a different element.\n\nChemical reactions mostly rearrange electrons and bonds. Ordinary chemistry does not routinely change atomic nuclei. That separation is important: electron structure explains much of chemical behavior, while nuclear changes belong to nuclear physics and chemistry.","worked_example":{"setup":"Compare neutral sodium with a sodium ion that has lost one electron.","steps":["Both have 11 protons, so both are sodium.","The ion has one fewer electron.","The changed electron count changes charge, not elemental identity."],"result":"Element identity follows proton number; charge follows the balance of protons and electrons."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain the roles of protons, neutrons, and electrons and why proton number defines the element.","challenge":"Explain what changes—and what does not—when an atom becomes a positive ion.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What determines the identity of an element?","options":["Number of protons","Number of molecules nearby","Temperature","Physical size"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"The atomic number—the number of protons—defines the element.","feedback":["The atomic number—the number of protons—defines the element.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"astronomy_solar_system":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"The Solar System: gravity organizing many kinds of worlds","objective":"Describe the major components of the Solar System and explain why gravity dominates their large-scale motion.","key_terms":[{"term":"Orbit","definition":"A path followed under gravity around another body."},{"term":"Planet","definition":"A large body orbiting the Sun that has cleared its orbital neighborhood."},{"term":"Asteroid","definition":"A relatively small rocky or metallic Solar System body."},{"term":"Comet","definition":"A small icy body that can develop a coma or tail near the Sun."}],"explanation":"The Solar System contains the Sun, eight planets, dwarf planets, moons, asteroids, comets, dust, and smaller bodies. The Sun contains most of the system's mass, so its gravity dominates planetary orbits.\n\nAn orbit is not a balance between 'no gravity' and motion. The orbiting object is continuously falling toward the central body while its sideways velocity keeps it from hitting it. The result is a curved path.\n\nThe inner planets are relatively small and rocky. The outer Solar System contains giant planets with very different compositions and extensive moon systems. Small-body populations preserve clues about Solar System formation, so asteroids and comets are scientifically important rather than leftovers with no value.","worked_example":{"setup":"Imagine Earth suddenly lost its sideways orbital velocity while the Sun's gravity remained.","steps":["Gravity would still pull Earth toward the Sun.","Without enough sideways motion, the orbit would not be maintained.","Earth would fall inward rather than continue its present orbit."],"result":"Orbit requires both gravity and appropriate motion."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Describe the major components of the Solar System and explain why gravity dominates their large-scale motion.","challenge":"Explain an orbit without using the phrase 'there is no gravity in space.'","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Why does Earth remain in orbit around the Sun?","options":["There is no gravity between them","Gravity continually bends Earth's moving path","The Moon pushes Earth around the Sun","Space is a solid track"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Earth's velocity carries it forward while solar gravity continually curves its path.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Earth's velocity carries it forward while solar gravity continually curves its path.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"earth_science_earth_structure":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Earth Structure: a layered, dynamic planet","objective":"Describe Earth's major internal layers and connect their properties to plate tectonics and the magnetic field.","key_terms":[{"term":"Crust","definition":"Thin outer rocky layer."},{"term":"Mantle","definition":"Thick rocky layer that can flow slowly over geologic time."},{"term":"Outer core","definition":"Liquid iron-rich layer."},{"term":"Inner core","definition":"Solid iron-rich central region."}],"explanation":"Earth is layered because materials separated and changed under enormous pressure and temperature. The crust is thin compared with the planet. Beneath it, the mantle is solid rock overall but can deform and flow slowly over long times.\n\nThe core is mostly iron-rich. The outer core is liquid, while the inner core is solid because the pressure is extremely high. Motion of electrically conducting liquid in the outer core helps generate Earth's magnetic field.\n\nPlate tectonics involves the rigid lithosphere—crust plus the uppermost mantle—moving over weaker underlying mantle. The movement is connected to Earth's internal heat and mantle dynamics. Earth is therefore not a static rock; it is an evolving thermal and mechanical system.","worked_example":{"setup":"Why can solid mantle rock participate in convection over geologic time?","steps":["Solid does not always mean perfectly rigid forever.","At high temperature and over millions of years, mantle rock can deform slowly.","Slow deformation permits large-scale material circulation."],"result":"A material can behave solidly on short timescales yet flow on geological timescales."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Describe Earth's major internal layers and connect their properties to plate tectonics and the magnetic field.","challenge":"Connect one internal Earth layer to a surface phenomenon such as earthquakes, volcanoes, or the magnetic field.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which Earth layer is liquid and important to the magnetic field?","options":["Crust","Mantle","Outer core","Inner core"],"correct_index":2,"rationale":"The liquid, electrically conducting outer core is central to the geodynamo.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","The liquid, electrically conducting outer core is central to the geodynamo.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"engineering_engineering_problem_definition":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Engineering Problem Definition: solve the right problem first","objective":"Turn a vague need into measurable requirements, constraints, and success criteria before choosing a solution.","key_terms":[{"term":"Requirement","definition":"What the solution must accomplish."},{"term":"Constraint","definition":"A limit the solution must respect."},{"term":"Criterion","definition":"A measure used to compare solutions."},{"term":"Stakeholder","definition":"A person or group affected by the solution."}],"explanation":"Engineering starts before calculations and drawings. A vague request such as 'make this room more comfortable' can hide several different problems: temperature variation, humidity, noise, airflow, control, or occupant expectations.\n\nA useful problem definition separates requirements from constraints. A requirement might be 'maintain 72–75°F during occupied hours.' Constraints might include existing electrical capacity, budget, space, code, schedule, and maintenance access.\n\nGood definitions also identify stakeholders and how success will be measured. If the team jumps to a favorite solution before defining the problem, it can optimize the wrong thing. Engineering is not only finding an answer; it is deciding what question the answer must satisfy.","worked_example":{"setup":"A warehouse says, 'The loading area is too hot.'","steps":["Measure when and where the problem occurs.","Define acceptable temperature or comfort targets.","Identify constraints such as doors, equipment heat, budget, and operating schedule.","Only then compare possible solutions."],"result":"The first engineering product is a clear problem definition, not a piece of equipment."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Turn a vague need into measurable requirements, constraints, and success criteria before choosing a solution.","challenge":"Take a vague real-world complaint and rewrite it as one requirement, two constraints, and one success criterion.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which is a constraint?","options":["Maintain 70–74°F","Existing electrical service cannot exceed 200 A","Reduce complaints","Improve comfort"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"A constraint limits the design space; available electrical capacity is a hard limit.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","A constraint limits the design space; available electrical capacity is a hard limit.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"computing_binary_and_data_representation":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Binary & Data Representation: how computers turn states into information","objective":"Explain how bits represent numbers and other data by assigning meaning to patterns of 0s and 1s.","key_terms":[{"term":"Bit","definition":"A binary digit with two possible states."},{"term":"Byte","definition":"Commonly eight bits."},{"term":"Binary","definition":"Base-2 positional number system."},{"term":"Encoding","definition":"Rules that map bit patterns to meaning."}],"explanation":"Digital computers use physical systems that can reliably distinguish states. We label two states 0 and 1, creating a bit. By combining bits, a computer can represent many patterns.\n\nBinary numbers use powers of two. The four-bit pattern 1010 means 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 0×1 = 10 in decimal. The same bit pattern could mean something else under a different encoding.\n\nThat last point matters: bits do not contain meaning by themselves. An encoding tells the system whether a pattern represents a number, character, color, machine instruction, or something else. Software works because layers of agreed rules interpret those patterns consistently.","worked_example":{"setup":"Convert binary 1101 to decimal.","steps":["Place values are 8, 4, 2, 1.","1101 means 8 + 4 + 0 + 1.","The total is 13."],"result":"Binary 1101 represents decimal 13 when interpreted as an unsigned base-2 integer."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain how bits represent numbers and other data by assigning meaning to patterns of 0s and 1s.","challenge":"Write the place values for a five-bit binary number and convert one example.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What is a bit?","options":["A decimal digit from 0 to 9","A binary state commonly represented as 0 or 1","Exactly 1,000 bytes","A type of monitor"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"A bit is the basic two-state unit used in binary representation.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","A bit is the basic two-state unit used in binary representation.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"history_early_civilizations":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Early Civilizations: why complex societies formed","objective":"Identify recurring features of early civilizations and explain how agriculture, surplus, institutions, and environment interacted.","key_terms":[{"term":"Surplus","definition":"Production beyond immediate consumption needs."},{"term":"State","definition":"Political organization exercising authority over a territory and population."},{"term":"Specialization","definition":"People concentrating on different occupations or roles."}],"explanation":"Early civilizations emerged in different places and did not follow one identical path. Many developed where agriculture could support larger, denser populations, including river valleys, but environment alone did not create civilization.\n\nAgricultural surplus could support people who were not full-time farmers—craft specialists, soldiers, administrators, priests, and traders. Larger settlements created coordination problems involving water, storage, defense, disputes, and public works. Institutions developed to organize these tasks.\n\nWriting, taxation, hierarchy, trade, and monumental construction appeared in some civilizations, but at different times and in different forms. The useful historical habit is to look for interacting causes rather than assuming one invention or one ruler created a complex society.","worked_example":{"setup":"A farming region begins producing reliable grain surpluses.","steps":["Some food can support people doing non-farming work.","Specialization and trade can expand.","Storage and distribution create administrative needs.","Political and religious institutions may become more complex."],"result":"Surplus can enable complexity, but it does not mechanically guarantee one type of civilization."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Identify recurring features of early civilizations and explain how agriculture, surplus, institutions, and environment interacted.","challenge":"Choose one early civilization and identify two interacting factors that supported its growth.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Why can agricultural surplus contribute to social specialization?","options":["It lets some people obtain food without farming full time","It eliminates all conflict","It makes government unnecessary","It prevents trade"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Surplus can support people working in crafts, administration, trade, defense, and other roles.","feedback":["Surplus can support people working in crafts, administration, trade, defense, and other roles.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"geography_continents_and_oceans":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Continents & Oceans: a spatial framework for the world","objective":"Use the major continents and oceans as a reference system for locating regions, routes, climates, and human activity.","key_terms":[{"term":"Continent","definition":"A very large landmass conventionally divided into major world regions."},{"term":"Ocean","definition":"A major interconnected body of salt water."},{"term":"Hemisphere","definition":"Half of Earth, divided by reference lines such as the equator or prime meridian."}],"explanation":"Geography becomes easier when you have a mental frame for where things are. Continents and oceans form that first frame. Their boundaries are partly physical and partly conventional, which is why different educational traditions sometimes count continents differently.\n\nThe oceans are physically connected, but names such as Atlantic, Pacific, Indian, Arctic, and Southern help describe location and circulation. Oceans strongly influence climate, transport, resources, and geopolitical access.\n\nA map is not the territory itself. Map projections distort some combination of area, distance, shape, or direction when the curved Earth is shown on a flat surface. Good geographic reasoning therefore combines location with scale, environment, movement, and human systems.","worked_example":{"setup":"Why can Western Europe have milder winters than some places at similar latitude in Canada?","steps":["Latitude matters, but it is not the only influence.","The Atlantic Ocean stores and transports heat.","Prevailing circulation and ocean-atmosphere interactions affect regional climate."],"result":"Location provides a framework; physical processes explain the pattern."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Use the major continents and oceans as a reference system for locating regions, routes, climates, and human activity.","challenge":"Pick a country and describe its continent, neighboring ocean or sea, and one way location affects it.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which ocean lies between the Americas and Europe/Africa?","options":["Pacific","Atlantic","Indian","Arctic only"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"The Atlantic Ocean separates the Americas from Europe and Africa.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","The Atlantic Ocean separates the Americas from Europe and Africa.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"geopolitics_state_power":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"State Power: what gives a country influence","objective":"Explain state power as a combination of resources, institutions, geography, capabilities, and relationships rather than one simple ranking.","key_terms":[{"term":"State","definition":"A political entity with governing authority over a territory and population."},{"term":"Capability","definition":"A resource or ability that can be used to pursue goals."},{"term":"Leverage","definition":"The ability to influence another actor's choices."}],"explanation":"Power in international relations is the ability to shape outcomes, but it is not one thing. Population, economic production, technology, military capacity, energy, geography, institutions, alliances, and political legitimacy can all matter.\n\nResources are not automatically usable power. A country may have large natural resources but weak infrastructure or institutions. Another may have limited territory but strong technology, finance, alliances, or control over important trade routes.\n\nPower is also relational. A capability matters because of who is interacting, what they want, and what alternatives exist. Military strength may matter greatly in one dispute and very little in another where finance, regulation, or supply chains dominate.","worked_example":{"setup":"A small country controls a narrow sea route used by a large share of regional shipping.","steps":["Its total economy may be modest.","Its geographic position creates leverage over a specific flow.","That leverage depends on alternatives, alliances, and the costs of disruption."],"result":"Power can be issue-specific rather than a single overall score."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain state power as a combination of resources, institutions, geography, capabilities, and relationships rather than one simple ranking.","challenge":"Choose one country and identify three different sources of power plus one limitation.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which statement about state power is strongest?","options":["Military size is the only meaningful form of power","Resources become influence depending on institutions, relationships, and context","Population alone determines every outcome","Small states can never have leverage"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Power depends on multiple capabilities and the context in which they can be used.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Power depends on multiple capabilities and the context in which they can be used.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"government_constitutional_principles":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Constitutional Principles: rules for who may exercise power","objective":"Explain how constitutionalism, separation of powers, checks and balances, and federalism structure U.S. government authority.","key_terms":[{"term":"Constitutionalism","definition":"Government power is limited and organized by higher legal rules."},{"term":"Separation of powers","definition":"Government functions are divided among branches."},{"term":"Checks and balances","definition":"Institutions have tools to limit or respond to one another."},{"term":"Federalism","definition":"Power is divided between national and state governments."}],"explanation":"A constitution does more than create offices. It establishes rules about where government power comes from, who may exercise it, and what limits apply.\n\nIn the United States, legislative, executive, and judicial powers are placed in different branches. The separation is not complete. Each branch has ways to affect the others: presidents can veto legislation, Congress controls appropriations and can override vetoes under specified conditions, and courts decide cases involving constitutional questions.\n\nFederalism adds another layer by dividing authority between national and state governments. Disagreement over boundaries is normal because constitutional government is a system of allocated and contested powers, not a machine in which every question has an obvious owner.","worked_example":{"setup":"Congress passes a bill, the President vetoes it, and Congress considers an override.","steps":["Congress exercised legislative power.","The President used an executive check.","The possible override is a legislative check on the veto."],"result":"Checks and balances create interaction rather than total branch independence."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain how constitutionalism, separation of powers, checks and balances, and federalism structure U.S. government authority.","challenge":"Pick one government action and identify which constitutional principle most affects it.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What is federalism?","options":["Rule by judges","Division of authority between national and state governments","A ban on elections","Complete independence of each branch"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Federalism divides governing authority across national and state levels.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Federalism divides governing authority across national and state levels.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"law_sources_of_law":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Sources of Law: where legal rules come from","objective":"Distinguish constitutions, statutes, regulations, and judicial decisions and explain how authority and jurisdiction matter.","key_terms":[{"term":"Constitution","definition":"Foundational law organizing government and limiting power."},{"term":"Statute","definition":"Law enacted by a legislature."},{"term":"Regulation","definition":"Rule issued by an authorized administrative agency."},{"term":"Precedent","definition":"A prior judicial decision used in later legal reasoning."}],"explanation":"Legal questions often begin with a source question: what rule has authority here? Constitutions sit at a high level of legal authority. Legislatures enact statutes. Administrative agencies issue regulations when law gives them authority to do so. Courts interpret and apply these sources in cases.\n\nThe sources interact. A regulation cannot validly exceed the authority granted by statute, and statutes are subject to constitutional limits. Judicial decisions may interpret ambiguous language and create precedent within the relevant legal system.\n\nJurisdiction matters. A rule from one state, court, or agency may not control a different jurisdiction. Legal reasoning therefore requires more than finding a rule that sounds relevant; you must ask who issued it, under what authority, and whether it governs the situation.","worked_example":{"setup":"A federal agency issues a workplace rule under authority granted by Congress.","steps":["Congress enacted the statute granting authority.","The agency issued a regulation under that authority.","A court could later review whether the rule fits the statute and Constitution."],"result":"Different legal sources have different roles and levels of authority."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Distinguish constitutions, statutes, regulations, and judicial decisions and explain how authority and jurisdiction matter.","challenge":"Take a familiar legal rule and identify whether it is constitutional, statutory, regulatory, or judicial in origin.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which source is enacted by a legislature?","options":["Statute","Regulation only","Private contract","Scientific theory"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A statute is legislation enacted through a legislative body.","feedback":["A statute is legislation enacted through a legislative body.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"finance_time_value_of_money":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Time Value of Money: why timing changes value","objective":"Explain why money available at different times can have different economic value and calculate a simple future value.","key_terms":[{"term":"Principal","definition":"The starting amount of money."},{"term":"Interest rate","definition":"The rate at which money grows or borrowing costs accumulate."},{"term":"Future value","definition":"What money today grows to in the future."},{"term":"Present value","definition":"What a future amount is worth in today's terms under a chosen rate."}],"explanation":"Start with a concrete choice: would you rather receive $100 today or $100 one year from now, if there is no reason to prefer waiting? Receiving it today gives you options. You can spend it, keep it available, or invest it.\n\nIf $100 can earn 5% over one year, it becomes $105. That means $100 today and $100 a year from now are not economically equivalent under that opportunity. The earlier money had time to grow.\n\nThis is the core of the time value of money. Later, finance adds inflation, risk, multiple cash flows, and discount rates, but those are additional layers. First learn the simple relationship: money can earn a return over time, so the timing of a cash flow matters.","worked_example":{"setup":"You receive $200 today and can earn 4% over one year.","steps":["Four percent of $200 is $8.","Add the return to the principal: $200 + $8.","Equivalent calculation: $200 × 1.04 = $208."],"result":"After one year, the future value is $208."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain why money available at different times can have different economic value and calculate a simple future value.","challenge":"If you could earn 6%, calculate what $500 today could become after one year.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"If $100 earns 5% for one year, what is its future value?","options":["$95","$100","$105","$150"],"correct_index":2,"rationale":"Five percent of $100 is $5, so the one-year future value is $105.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Five percent of $100 is $5, so the one-year future value is $105.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"business_business_models":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Business Models: how an organization creates and captures value","objective":"Describe a business model using customers, value proposition, revenue, costs, and key activities.","key_terms":[{"term":"Customer","definition":"The person or organization served."},{"term":"Value proposition","definition":"The reason a customer chooses the offering."},{"term":"Revenue model","definition":"How the business gets paid."},{"term":"Cost structure","definition":"The major costs required to operate."}],"explanation":"A business model explains how an organization creates value for someone and captures enough value to sustain itself. A good product is only one part of the model.\n\nStart with the customer and the problem being solved. Then ask what the business must do to deliver the solution, how customers pay, and what the major costs are. A service company may earn revenue per job while carrying labor, vehicles, insurance, tools, and overhead.\n\nTwo companies can sell similar products with very different models. One may sell equipment once; another may lease it with ongoing service. The economics, customer relationship, cash flow, and risk can therefore be very different even when the physical product looks similar.","worked_example":{"setup":"Compare an HVAC contractor that sells replacements with one that also sells maintenance memberships.","steps":["Both create value by maintaining indoor comfort.","Replacement jobs generate episodic revenue.","Memberships create recurring revenue and ongoing customer contact.","The second model changes scheduling, staffing, sales, and customer-lifetime economics."],"result":"The business model connects the offering to the way the company actually operates and earns money."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Describe a business model using customers, value proposition, revenue, costs, and key activities.","challenge":"Describe a familiar business using customer, value proposition, revenue model, and two major costs.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which question belongs in a business model?","options":["How does the organization get paid?","What is the atomic number of carbon?","What is Earth's orbital period?","Which branch interprets laws?"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Revenue is a core part of how a business captures value.","feedback":["Revenue is a core part of how a business captures value.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"leadership_goal_setting":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Goal Setting: turning direction into executable work","objective":"Write goals that connect a desired outcome to measurable evidence, constraints, ownership, and a time horizon.","key_terms":[{"term":"Outcome","definition":"The result you want to achieve."},{"term":"Metric","definition":"A measure used to track progress."},{"term":"Constraint","definition":"A limit or condition that matters."},{"term":"Milestone","definition":"An intermediate point showing progress."}],"explanation":"A vague goal such as 'improve service' gives direction but not enough information to manage work. A useful goal says what result should change, how you will know, and over what time period.\n\nMetrics should connect to the outcome rather than reward activity for its own sake. 'Make 100 calls' measures activity. 'Reduce average scheduling delay from five days to three days without increasing callbacks' is closer to an operational outcome.\n\nGoals also need ownership and tradeoffs. Improving speed while quality collapses is not success. Good leaders use goals to align decisions, then review evidence and adjust when assumptions prove wrong.","worked_example":{"setup":"A service manager wants faster response times.","steps":["Define the current baseline.","Choose a target such as reducing median response time from 48 to 30 hours.","Add a quality guardrail such as no increase in repeat visits.","Assign ownership and review progress weekly."],"result":"The goal becomes a decision tool instead of a slogan."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Write goals that connect a desired outcome to measurable evidence, constraints, ownership, and a time horizon.","challenge":"Rewrite one vague goal you have into an outcome, metric, deadline, and quality guardrail.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which goal is most measurable?","options":["Do better","Improve customer service someday","Reduce average callback rate from 8% to 5% by December","Work harder"],"correct_index":2,"rationale":"It defines a metric, target, and time horizon.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","It defines a metric, target, and time horizon.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"psychology_learning_and_conditioning":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Learning & Conditioning: how experience changes behavior","objective":"Distinguish classical conditioning from operant conditioning and identify reinforcement and punishment.","key_terms":[{"term":"Classical conditioning","definition":"Learning an association between stimuli."},{"term":"Operant conditioning","definition":"Learning from consequences of behavior."},{"term":"Reinforcement","definition":"A consequence that increases a behavior."},{"term":"Punishment","definition":"A consequence that decreases a behavior."}],"explanation":"Psychology uses conditioning to describe ways experience can change behavior. In classical conditioning, one stimulus comes to predict another. A sound repeatedly paired with food can eventually trigger a response associated with food.\n\nOperant conditioning focuses on consequences. If a consequence makes a behavior more likely in the future, it is reinforcement. If it makes the behavior less likely, it is punishment. 'Positive' and 'negative' mean adding or removing something, not good or bad.\n\nFor example, fastening a seat belt to stop an annoying warning sound can be negative reinforcement: removing the sound increases seat-belt use. The vocabulary matters because everyday meanings of positive, negative, reinforcement, and punishment can be misleading.","worked_example":{"setup":"A technician completes required documentation and immediately receives useful schedule flexibility.","steps":["The behavior is completing documentation.","The consequence is added flexibility.","If documentation increases, the consequence functioned as reinforcement."],"result":"Conditioning is defined by how behavior changes, not by whether the consequence sounds pleasant."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Distinguish classical conditioning from operant conditioning and identify reinforcement and punishment.","challenge":"Create one example of reinforcement and explain why it should increase a behavior.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which definition of reinforcement is correct?","options":["A consequence that increases a behavior","Any unpleasant consequence","A consequence that always involves praise","A behavior that disappears"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Reinforcement is defined by increasing future behavior.","feedback":["Reinforcement is defined by increasing future behavior.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"sociology_social_structure":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Social Structure: patterns larger than one individual","objective":"Explain how roles, institutions, networks, and unequal access to resources shape repeated social patterns.","key_terms":[{"term":"Role","definition":"Expected behavior associated with a social position."},{"term":"Institution","definition":"Durable system organizing major areas of social life."},{"term":"Network","definition":"Pattern of social relationships."},{"term":"Structure","definition":"Repeated social arrangements that shape opportunities and constraints."}],"explanation":"Sociology looks at patterns that cannot be explained only by individual personality. People act within families, workplaces, schools, markets, governments, communities, and networks that create expectations and opportunities.\n\nA role is a set of expectations attached to a position. Institutions are larger, durable arrangements such as education, law, family, or the economy. Social structure describes the patterned relationships among these elements.\n\nStructure does not mean people have no choice. It means choices occur within conditions that are not equally distributed. Two people can make similar decisions but face different networks, resources, rules, or risks. Sociology studies the interaction between individual action and these larger patterns.","worked_example":{"setup":"Two equally skilled workers look for jobs, but one has a large professional network and the other does not.","steps":["Individual skill matters.","Network access changes information and opportunities.","The outcome reflects both individual characteristics and social structure."],"result":"Social explanations can include individual choices without reducing everything to individual choice."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain how roles, institutions, networks, and unequal access to resources shape repeated social patterns.","challenge":"Describe one situation where a social role, institution, or network influences what people can do.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What is a social institution?","options":["A durable system organizing an area of social life","One person's mood","A chemical element","A weather event"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Institutions are durable organized patterns such as education, law, family, or markets.","feedback":["Institutions are durable organized patterns such as education, law, family, or markets.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"trades_mechanical_construction_drawings":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Construction Drawings: reading the building before touching the building","objective":"Use plans, elevations, sections, symbols, dimensions, and schedules together to understand what is intended to be built.","key_terms":[{"term":"Plan view","definition":"A horizontal view looking down through the building."},{"term":"Elevation","definition":"A vertical view of a face or wall."},{"term":"Section","definition":"A cut-through view showing relationships hidden in a plan."},{"term":"Schedule","definition":"A table of repeated component information such as equipment, doors, or finishes."},{"term":"Scale","definition":"The relationship between drawing size and real-world size."}],"explanation":"A construction drawing is not one picture of a building. It is a coordinated set of views and notes that describe different parts of the same physical system. The first skill is learning what question each drawing type can answer.\n\nA plan view is like looking down after making a horizontal cut through the building. It is useful for layout, locations, walls, rooms, duct routes, piping routes, and equipment positions. An elevation looks horizontally at a vertical face. A section cuts through the building so you can see heights, layers, clearances, and relationships that a plan can hide.\n\nSymbols and abbreviations keep drawings readable, but they only work if you use the legend and drawing notes. Dimensions should normally control over measuring a printed or on-screen drawing, because copies can be resized. Scale is useful when a dimension is not explicitly given, but only after confirming the drawing scale.\n\nDrawings also reference one another. A plan may contain a section marker that points to another sheet. An equipment tag may point to a schedule containing capacity, model information, electrical data, or connection sizes. Reading drawings well means following those references instead of trying to force one sheet to contain the whole answer.\n\nFor field work, always distinguish design intent from actual field conditions. Existing buildings can differ from drawings, revisions can supersede older sheets, and coordination among architectural, structural, mechanical, electrical, and plumbing drawings matters before installation.","worked_example":{"setup":"You need to determine whether a supply duct can pass above a corridor ceiling without hitting structure.","steps":["Start on the mechanical plan to locate the duct route.","Use section/elevation references to determine vertical position and ceiling/structure relationships.","Check structural drawings for beams or other conflicts.","Verify dimensions, notes, and the current revision before treating the path as buildable."],"result":"No single view answers the whole coordination question; you combine views and disciplines."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Use plans, elevations, sections, symbols, dimensions, and schedules together to understand what is intended to be built.","challenge":"Pick one item on a construction drawing—duct, pipe, wall, equipment, door—and list which drawing views or schedules you would use to verify its location and size.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"A plan shows a section marker beside a duct crossing. What is the best next move if you need to know vertical clearance?","options":["Measure the plan with a ruler and assume the height","Open the referenced section because it can show vertical relationships","Ignore the marker","Use only the title block"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"A section is specifically useful for seeing vertical relationships that the plan view may not show.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","A section is specifically useful for seeing vertical relationships that the plan view may not show.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"philosophy_logic_foundations":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Logic Foundations: when does a conclusion follow?","objective":"Distinguish premises from conclusions and evaluate whether reasoning supports a conclusion.","key_terms":[{"term":"Premise","definition":"A statement offered as support."},{"term":"Conclusion","definition":"The claim the reasoning is trying to establish."},{"term":"Validity","definition":"Whether a deductive conclusion must follow if the premises are true."},{"term":"Soundness","definition":"Validity plus true premises."}],"explanation":"Logic studies the structure of reasoning. An argument is not necessarily a fight; it is a set of premises offered in support of a conclusion.\n\nIn deductive reasoning, validity asks a conditional question: if the premises were true, would the conclusion have to be true? A valid argument can still have a false conclusion if one of its premises is false. Soundness requires both valid structure and true premises.\n\nThis separates two kinds of criticism. You can challenge the logic connecting premises to conclusion, or you can challenge the truth of a premise. Keeping those separate makes disagreements clearer and prevents 'the conclusion sounds wrong' from substituting for analysis.","worked_example":{"setup":"Premise 1: All mammals are warm-blooded. Premise 2: Whales are mammals. Conclusion: Whales are warm-blooded.","steps":["Assume the premises are true.","The category relationship forces the conclusion.","The argument is deductively valid."],"result":"If the premises are true, the conclusion follows necessarily."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Distinguish premises from conclusions and evaluate whether reasoning supports a conclusion.","challenge":"Write a two-premise argument and identify its premises and conclusion.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What is a premise?","options":["A statement offered as support for a conclusion","The final claim only","A punctuation mark","An emotion"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A premise is a supporting statement in an argument.","feedback":["A premise is a supporting statement in an argument.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"religion_culture_judaism_foundations":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Judaism Foundations: text, practice, peoplehood, and historical development","objective":"Describe Judaism as a diverse religious and cultural tradition shaped by covenant, sacred texts, law, practice, community, and history.","key_terms":[{"term":"Torah","definition":"In a narrow sense, the first five books of the Hebrew Bible; the word also has broader uses in Jewish tradition."},{"term":"Tanakh","definition":"The Hebrew Bible, traditionally organized as Torah, Prophets, and Writings."},{"term":"Halakha","definition":"Jewish legal and practical tradition governing many areas of life."},{"term":"Diaspora","definition":"Communities living outside an ancestral homeland."}],"explanation":"Judaism is an ancient and internally diverse tradition. It includes religious belief and practice, shared texts and legal traditions, historical memory, community life, and forms of Jewish peoplehood and identity.\n\nThe Torah is foundational, and the wider Tanakh contains other biblical writings. Rabbinic literature—including the Mishnah and Talmud—became central to later Jewish law, interpretation, and practice. Jewish life developed across many regions and historical periods, especially through diaspora communities.\n\nThere is no single modern Jewish practice that represents every Jew. Orthodox, Conservative/Masorti, Reform/Progressive, secular, cultural, and other Jewish identities differ in theology, law, ritual, and community practice. Good comparative study therefore begins with foundational concepts while preserving internal diversity and historical context.","worked_example":{"setup":"Two Jewish communities observe the same holiday differently.","steps":["They may share historical texts and symbols.","Different legal traditions, local histories, denominations, or community customs can shape practice.","Variation does not mean one community is not Jewish."],"result":"A tradition can have shared foundations and substantial internal diversity."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Describe Judaism as a diverse religious and cultural tradition shaped by covenant, sacred texts, law, practice, community, and history.","challenge":"Choose one Jewish practice or text and explain how historical context or community tradition can affect how it is understood.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which statement is strongest?","options":["All Jews practice identically","Judaism includes shared foundations and substantial internal diversity","Judaism began in the modern era","The Torah has no role in Judaism"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Judaism has shared texts and traditions but diverse interpretations, practices, and identities.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Judaism has shared texts and traditions but diverse interpretations, practices, and identities.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"literature_narrative_structure":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Narrative Structure: how stories organize change","objective":"Identify setup, conflict, turning points, climax, and resolution and explain how structure shapes meaning.","key_terms":[{"term":"Conflict","definition":"A struggle or tension driving action."},{"term":"Turning point","definition":"An event that changes the direction of the story."},{"term":"Climax","definition":"A major point of decisive tension or change."},{"term":"Resolution","definition":"What follows from the central conflict and changes."}],"explanation":"Narrative structure is the pattern by which a story presents events and change. Many stories introduce a situation, create tension or conflict, develop consequences, reach important turning points, and then show some form of resolution.\n\nStructure is not a rigid formula. A story can begin near the end, use flashbacks, leave the conflict unresolved, or contain several climaxes. The useful question is not 'Does it follow the template?' but 'How does the ordering of events affect what the reader knows, expects, and feels?'\n\nA turning point matters because it changes possibilities. If a character learns a hidden fact, loses an ally, or makes an irreversible decision, the story's direction changes. Structure helps us explain why an event matters rather than simply listing what happened.","worked_example":{"setup":"A mystery reveals the identity of the victim's secret business partner halfway through the story.","steps":["Before the reveal, the reader interprets clues one way.","The new information changes which explanations are plausible.","Later scenes gain different meaning because the narrative possibilities changed."],"result":"The reveal functions as a turning point because it changes the story's direction and the reader's model."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Identify setup, conflict, turning points, climax, and resolution and explain how structure shapes meaning.","challenge":"Choose a movie, book, or story you know and identify one turning point plus what changed because of it.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What makes an event a turning point?","options":["It changes the direction or possibilities of the narrative","It contains the longest sentence","It must occur on page 50","It always ends the story"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"A turning point changes what can happen or how the situation is understood.","feedback":["A turning point changes what can happen or how the situation is understood.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"art_music_elements_of_visual_art":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Elements of Visual Art: the basic ingredients of an image","objective":"Use line, shape, form, value, color, texture, and space to describe how a visual work is constructed.","key_terms":[{"term":"Line","definition":"A path or edge that can guide the eye or define form."},{"term":"Value","definition":"Relative lightness or darkness."},{"term":"Texture","definition":"Actual or implied surface quality."},{"term":"Space","definition":"The organization of positive and negative areas and perceived depth."}],"explanation":"Visual analysis becomes easier when you separate what an image depicts from how it is built. Artists organize line, shape, form, color, value, texture, and space to direct attention and create relationships.\n\nValue can make a flat shape appear three-dimensional by suggesting light and shadow. Lines can outline objects, imply motion, or guide the viewer's eye. Color can create contrast, harmony, emphasis, or atmosphere.\n\nThese elements are not meanings by themselves. A red shape does not have one universal message. Meaning depends on context, culture, subject, composition, and the relationship among elements. The elements give you vocabulary for describing what is actually present before jumping to interpretation.","worked_example":{"setup":"A portrait uses a very bright face against a dark background.","steps":["The strong value contrast draws attention to the face.","The dark surrounding space reduces competition.","The composition guides the viewer's eye before any symbolic interpretation."],"result":"Formal elements shape visual attention and can support meaning."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Use line, shape, form, value, color, texture, and space to describe how a visual work is constructed.","challenge":"Choose an image around you and describe it using three visual elements before interpreting what it means.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which term describes lightness and darkness in visual art?","options":["Value","Tempo","Voltage","Syntax"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Value refers to relative lightness and darkness.","feedback":["Value refers to relative lightness and darkness.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"media_source_evaluation":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Source Evaluation: deciding how much trust a claim deserves","objective":"Evaluate a source by separating the claim, evidence, expertise, incentives, corroboration, and uncertainty.","key_terms":[{"term":"Claim","definition":"A statement that can be supported or challenged."},{"term":"Primary source","definition":"Direct evidence from the event, data, document, or original research."},{"term":"Corroboration","definition":"Independent evidence supporting the same conclusion."},{"term":"Conflict of interest","definition":"An incentive that could affect judgment or presentation."}],"explanation":"Source evaluation is not the same as labeling websites 'good' or 'bad.' Start with the specific claim. A source can be reliable about one thing and weak about another.\n\nAsk what evidence the source provides and whether the source has relevant access or expertise. A company's press release is a strong primary source for what the company officially announced, but a weaker independent source for whether its product is the best on the market.\n\nThen look for corroboration. Independent sources using different evidence are more informative than many articles repeating the same original claim. Also examine date, context, methodology, uncertainty, and incentives. Credibility is a conclusion built from evidence, not a badge attached permanently to a domain.","worked_example":{"setup":"A company says its new product cuts energy use by 40%.","steps":["The company is a primary source for its own claim.","Its financial incentive means the performance claim needs independent evidence.","Look for test methods, baseline conditions, and independent measurements."],"result":"The source is useful, but the claim deserves evidence beyond the seller's statement."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Evaluate a source by separating the claim, evidence, expertise, incentives, corroboration, and uncertainty.","challenge":"Take one online claim and list what evidence would increase or decrease your confidence in it.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Which is the strongest corroboration?","options":["Ten sites copying the same press release","An independent test using a transparent method","A comment with no evidence","The source repeating its claim"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Independent evidence using a transparent method provides genuine corroboration.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Independent evidence using a transparent method provides genuine corroboration.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"energy_energy_forms":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Energy Forms: one conserved quantity, many useful descriptions","objective":"Identify common energy forms and track conversions without treating energy as something that appears from nowhere.","key_terms":[{"term":"Kinetic energy","definition":"Energy associated with motion."},{"term":"Potential energy","definition":"Energy associated with position or configuration."},{"term":"Thermal energy","definition":"Microscopic energy associated with particle motion and interactions."},{"term":"Chemical energy","definition":"Energy associated with molecular and electronic arrangements."}],"explanation":"Energy is a conserved physical quantity that can be transferred and transformed. We use names such as kinetic, gravitational potential, chemical, electrical, and thermal energy to describe where energy is stored or how it appears in a system.\n\nA battery powering a motor is a chain of conversions. Chemical processes in the battery create electrical energy transfer. The motor converts part of that electrical energy into mechanical motion. Some energy becomes thermal energy because real devices have resistance and friction.\n\nEfficiency asks how much input energy becomes the desired useful output. Energy that becomes heat is not destroyed; it has moved into a form that may be less useful for the task. Tracking the full energy path helps explain why no real machine can produce more energy than it receives.","worked_example":{"setup":"An electric fan runs from a battery.","steps":["Chemical energy in the battery drives electrical current.","The motor converts electrical input into rotational kinetic energy.","Electrical resistance and friction also produce heat and sound."],"result":"Energy changes form and location; it is not created by the fan."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Identify common energy forms and track conversions without treating energy as something that appears from nowhere.","challenge":"Trace the energy transformations in one device you use every day.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"What happens to energy in an inefficient motor?","options":["The missing energy vanishes","Some input becomes less-useful heat and other outputs","The motor creates extra energy","Efficiency can exceed 100% indefinitely"],"correct_index":1,"rationale":"Energy is conserved; inefficiency means more input goes to undesired forms such as heat.","feedback":["Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Energy is conserved; inefficiency means more input goes to undesired forms such as heat.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}},"technology_semiconductors":{"provider":"atlas_core","content_status":"ready","mode":"lesson","modality":"human_tutor_core","suggested_minutes":12,"title":"Semiconductors: controlling electrical behavior","objective":"Explain why semiconductor conductivity can be controlled and how that makes switching and sensing possible.","key_terms":[{"term":"Semiconductor","definition":"Material whose electrical behavior can be engineered between typical conductors and insulators."},{"term":"Doping","definition":"Adding controlled impurities to change charge-carrier behavior."},{"term":"Transistor","definition":"Semiconductor device used to switch or amplify signals."},{"term":"Charge carrier","definition":"An electron or hole that contributes to electrical conduction."}],"explanation":"Semiconductors such as silicon are useful because their electrical conductivity can be controlled. Pure silicon is not as conductive as a metal, but its behavior changes when small amounts of other atoms are introduced through doping.\n\nDoping can create regions where electrons or 'holes' act as the main charge carriers. Joining differently doped regions creates structures whose current depends on voltage and electric fields. Those structures are the basis of diodes and transistors.\n\nA transistor can act as a controllable switch. Modern processors contain enormous numbers of transistors switching electrical states to implement logic. The same semiconductor principles also support sensors, solar cells, power electronics, and communication devices.","worked_example":{"setup":"Think of a transistor as a controlled electrical path.","steps":["A small control signal changes conditions inside the semiconductor.","That change can allow or restrict a larger current path.","Combining many such switches creates digital logic."],"result":"The key feature is controllability, not simply that silicon conducts electricity."},"common_mistakes":[],"takeaway":"Explain why semiconductor conductivity can be controlled and how that makes switching and sensing possible.","challenge":"Explain why an ordinary metal wire cannot replace every transistor in a processor.","hint":"Reduce the problem to the smallest idea you are sure about, then build one step at a time.","quiz":{"prompt":"Why are semiconductors especially useful in electronics?","options":["Their conductivity can be engineered and controlled","They always conduct better than copper","They contain no electrons","They eliminate heat"],"correct_index":0,"rationale":"Controlled electrical behavior lets semiconductor devices switch, amplify, sense, and convert energy.","feedback":["Controlled electrical behavior lets semiconductor devices switch, amplify, sense, and convert energy.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example.","Not quite. Rebuild the idea from the concrete example."]}}};
